(window.PF_CAT_SHARD=window.PF_CAT_SHARD||{})["76455"]={"2438":{"i":"2438","entryRequirements":"Candidates for the degree of Master of Professional Practice shall before enrolment have satisfied all the normal requirements for entry to the Institute and either:  \n\r\ngained an appropriate NZQA approved Bachelor's degree or Bachelor's degree with Honours; or\n\r\n*\t(MPP (Leadership)) Have successfully completed the Postgraduate Diploma in Professional Practice and Leadership or an equivalent postgraduate diploma from another institution.\n\r\n*\t(MPP (Trauma-Responsive Therapy)) Have successfully completed the Diploma in Professional Practice (Trauma-Responsive Therapy) with a minimum average grade of 75% (B+) or an equivalent postgraduate diploma in a closely related field from another institution.\n\r\n*\t(MPP (Trauma-Informed Leadership and Service)) Have successfully completed the Postgraduate Diploma in Trauma-Informed Leadership and Service with a minimum average grade of 75% (B+) or an equivalent postgraduate diploma in a closely related field from another institution.\n\n\n\r\nAn additional entry requirement for the MPP (Trauma-Responsive Therapy) is that applicants must supply evidence of provisional or full membership of an appropriate professional body with supervised practice experience in responding to trauma.","strategicPurposeStatement":"The Master of Professional Practice (with majors in Leadership, Trauma-Responsive Therapy, and Trauma-Informed Leadership and Service)) enables the development of the dispositions, as well as advanced level knowledge and skills to engage in research-informed professional practice which is understood as an expression of a call of the biblical narrative. Graduates further develop the capacity to engage in embodied scholarship that is informed by faith, inspired by hope and motivated by love, to undertake supervised independent research, to honour ethical principles central to human flourishing, including those derived from Te Tiriti o Waitangi, and those pertaining to culturally responsive practice, and to contribute as leaders to the wider professional community.","graduateProfile":"Graduates will:  \n\r\nHarmonise their professional, personal, cultural and spiritual identes to catalyse and sustain professional growth.  \n\r\nEngage with multiple perspectives with humility, empathy and grace.  \n\r\nDraw upon their faith and spirituality in a way that is a meaningful and integral part of their professional work. \n\r\nUtilise a reflective researcher-practitioner stance as a foundation for excellence in evidence-based professional practice. \n\n\n\r\n(Leadership) Critique the various benefits and challenges of different leadership approaches and locate oneself within this landscape. \n\r\n(Trauma-Responsive Therapy) Apply evidence-based strategies in ethically aware and culturally-attuned trauma therapy \n\r\n(Trauma-Informed Leadership and Service) Critique the various benefits and challenges of different trauma-informed leadership approaches and locate oneself within this landscape.","educationPathway":"Graduates may be able to apply for entry into doctoral studies.","developedBy":"Bethlehem Tertiary Institute","qualityAssuredBy":"New Zealand Qualifications Authority","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=2438"},"2439":{"i":"2439","entryRequirements":"1. Candidates for the Postgraduate Diploma in Professional Practice (Leadership) shall before enrolment have: \n\r\na. Satisfied all the normal requirements for entry to the Institute. \n\r\nb. Gained an appropriate NZQA approved Bachelor degree or Bachelor's degree with Honours. \n\n\n\r\nIn the event of a student without a Bachelor's degree, and a strong recommendation from the Interview panel, a student could be offered provisional entry. Continuation in the programme would be dependent on the student passing MPP840: Personal and Professional Principles in Practice with a B or above grade.\n\r\n a. A strong alignment with values of social change, justice, public good and people's well-being \n\r\nb. A minimum of two years' professional experience \n\r\nc. Evidence of belonging to a profession (e.g., have provisional or full registration with a professional registration board, have membership of a professional association).\n\r\n d. A letter of support from their immediate supervisor/manager indicating that they are employed or volunteering in an existing vocational situation which will provide a context for study and application of learning. \n\n\n\r\nIn keeping with NZQA guidelines, where English is not the student's first language, or who did not undertake their undergraduate studies in English they must demonstrate English language skills equivalent to an IELTS overall band score (academic) of 6.5, with no band score less than 6.0.","strategicPurposeStatement":"The BTI Postgraduate Diploma in Professional Practice (Leadership) enables the development of the dispositions, as well as advanced level knowledge and skills to engage in research-informed professional practice which is understood as an expression of a call of the biblical narrative. Graduates further develop the capacity to engage in embodied scholarship that is informed by faith, inspired by hope and motivated by love, to undertake supervised independent research, to honour ethical principles central to human flourishing, including those derived from Te Tiriti o Waitangi, and those pertaining to culturally responsive practice, and to contribute as leaders to the wider professional community. The Postgraduate Diploma in Professional Practice (Leadership) helps Christ-following professionals-- such as teachers, counsellors, social workers, mentors, chaplains, youth workers, pastors, occupational therapists and organisational leaders -- understand their leadership as missional and transformative, aiming for shalom (wellbeing and flourishing). Through advanced and imaginative exploration of faith's intellectual and practical implications, it aims to reconnect leadership with the Kingdom of God and the self in the socio-cultural context. The programme offers a rigorous, research-rich experience to nurture confident, Christ-following leaders whose practice aligns with their vision for individual and social wellbeing.","graduateProfile":"Graduates will:  \n\r\n* Harmonise their professional, personal, cultural and spiritual identities to catalyse and sustain professional growth.  \n\r\n* Engage with multiple perspectives with humility, empathy and grace.  \n\r\n* Draw upon their faith and spirituality in a way that is a meaningful and integral part of their professional work  \n\r\n* Consider the various benefits and challenges of different leadership approaches and locate oneself within this landscape.","educationPathway":"Graduates will have the capacity  to proceed to further postgraduate study.","developedBy":"Bethlehem Tertiary Institute","qualityAssuredBy":"New Zealand Qualifications Authority","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=2439"},"2798":{"i":"2798","entryRequirements":"Students must hold a NZQA equivalent level 7 degree or teaching diploma and be working in an early childhood service or in an organisation that has an early childhood education related focus.\n\r\nStudents holding a level 7 teaching diploma from an approved institution for initial teacher education, require current teacher registration and evidence of at least three years of professional, practical or scholarly experience of an appropriate kind. Admission is at the discretion of NZCA.","strategicPurposeStatement":"The postgraduate certificate is a first step into postgraduate study. It extends and deepens an individual's knowledge and skills by building on attainment in the principal subject(s) of the qualifying degree in education, early childhood education/teaching and/or leadership. It prepares an individual for further postgraduate study by staircasing into NZCA's Postgraduate Diploma in Leadership (ECE).","graduateProfile":"Postgraduate Certificate in Leadership (ECE) will have particularly strengthened their knowledge of leading and supporting teams, coaching and mentoring and leading pedagogical change within teams.","educationPathway":"graduates of this qualification can continue on to enrol in our Postgraduate Diploma in Leadership (ECE). They can also apply to cross-credit the two courses when enrolling in our Master of Education (ECE) programme.","developedBy":"Te Rito Maioha Early Childhood New Zealand Incorporated","qualityAssuredBy":"New Zealand Qualifications Authority","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=2798"},"2799":{"i":"2799","entryRequirements":"NZQA equivalent Level 7 teaching degree or teaching diploma and be working in an early childhood service or in an organisation that has an early childhood education-related focus.  Students holding a L7 teaching diploma must also have current teacher registration and evidence of at least 3 years of professional, practical or scholarly experience of an appropriate kind.  Admission is at the discretion of NZCA.","strategicPurposeStatement":"The overall aim of the programme is to increase the quality of early childhood education (ECE) by strenthening leadership capabilities for the benefit of children and families within the ECE and wider community. The programme also aims to: Apply critical awareness to effective pedagogical and organisational leadership; Demonstrate leaderful actions that make explicit commitment to bicultural partnerships in ECE; Critique leadership praxis in the ECE sector to facilitate educational, organisational and social change that is sustainable.","graduateProfile":"Graduates of the Postgraduate Diploma in Leadership (ECE) will deepen their knowledge of pedagogical, organisational and team leadership. Critical appraisal of self-identity and practice as a leader will enhance their capacity as transformative leaders who are able to effect the changes required to meet the challenges of leadership in the global early childhood sector in the 21st century and, in particular, within the special context of Aotearoa/New Zealand.","educationPathway":"Graduates will be able to seek employment in senior management and leadership roles in ECE.","developedBy":"Te Rito Maioha Early Childhood New Zealand Incorporated","qualityAssuredBy":"New Zealand Qualifications Authority","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=2799"},"3121":{"i":"3121","entryRequirements":"Applicants for the Postgraduate Certificate must have a Bachelor degree at Level 7 (360 credits) or higher qualification. Applicants for whom English is a second language and Te Reo Maori is not their first language must provide evidence of an Academic IELTS score issued within the last two years of at least 6.5 overall, 6.5 in writing with no band score lower than 6 or equivalent. IELTS scores may be combined from two tests as long as on one test a minimum of 6.5 overall and 6.0 in each component is achieved.","strategicPurposeStatement":"The Post Graduate Certificate in Education (Leadership and Management) aims to grow leaders and managers in the field of early childhood education. The qualification explores in-depth the convergences and divergences between leadership, mentoring and management, to support understanding of the role of a range of leaders in the early childhood profession. \n\r\nStudents learn to: \n\r\n* articulate what it means to be an authentic leader in the early childhood sector \n\r\n* critique foundational leadership approaches and evaluate their relevance for the early childhood sector \n\r\n* engage with the ethical basis of leadership \n\r\n* foster communities of learning within an early childhood context \n\r\n* develop and maintain a critically reflective stance in order to further inform their learning \n\r\n* critically analyse key management processes to ensure that knowledge and understanding is developed for mentoring teaching professionals in the early childhood sector in a sustainable manner","graduateProfile":"The Graduate profile is based on the Post Graduate Conceptual Framework. \n\r\nOn completion of this qualification graduates will: \n\r\n* be able to critique a range of leadership and management aspects and issues in early childhood contexts \n\r\n* have an in-depth understanding of leadership approaches including the knowledge, skills and dispositions required to be both an ethical and authentic leader \n\r\n* be able to relate the significance of the knowledge gained in the program to the context of early childhood education \n\r\n* have a sound understanding of critical management processes required to operate an organisation \n\r\n* have developed knowledge and skills in mentoring adults and building a professional early childhood community of learners \n\r\n* be well-informed in respect to leading and sustaining an organisational culture that fosters growth","educationPathway":"Graduates of the Postgraduate Certificate in Education (Leadership and Management) are able to pathway into the Postgraduate Diploma in Education (Early Childhood Education) or a Master's program, such as the proposed Master of Early Childhood Education or the Master of Education (Early Childhood Education).","developedBy":"New Zealand Tertiary College Limited","qualityAssuredBy":"New Zealand Qualifications Authority","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=3121"},"3122":{"i":"3122","entryRequirements":"Applicants for the Master of Early Childhood Education must have a Bachelor degree at Level 7 (360 credits) or higher qualification. Applicants for whom English is a second language and Te Reo Maori is not their first language must provide evidence of an Academic IELTS score issued within the last two years of at least 6.5 overall, 6.5 in writing with no band score lower than 6 or equivalent. IELTS scores may be combined from two tests as long as on one test a minimum of 6.5 overall and 6.0 in each component is achieved.","strategicPurposeStatement":"The aim of the Master of Early Childhood Education is to grow professionals into exemplary practitioners in early childhood education. The qualification provides a context in which students advance and extend their professional knowledge and skills through:\n\r\nCritical engagement, research and scholarship in a range of contemporary early childhood education topics and discourse;\n\r\nIndependent thinking and critical consciousness';\n\r\nSelf-reflexivity of teaching and learning pedagogies;\n\r\nResearch and critical reflection on practice as it relates to the field of early childhood education.","graduateProfile":"The graduate profile is based on the Postgraduate Conceptual Framework. On completion of this qualification, graduates will demonstrate:\n\r\nAdvanced theoretical knowledge fundamental to the early childhood sector;\n\r\nAcademic scholarship in a range of specialised early childhood topics;\n\r\nHigher order skills in critical thinking and independent thought; \n\r\nIn-depth critical reflection and research skills.","educationPathway":"Graduates of the Master of Early Childhood Education are able to pathway into a Master's programme such as the Master of Education (ECE) at NZTC to complete a thesis and be eligible to apply for a doctoral programme in education at a university.","developedBy":"New Zealand Tertiary College Limited","qualityAssuredBy":"New Zealand Qualifications Authority","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=3122"},"3345":{"i":"3345","entryRequirements":"Students must:\n\r\n* Be able to access a relevant workplace; and\n\r\n* Have a Bachelor degree or Graduate Certificate or Graduate Diploma in a cognate subject; or\n\r\n* Postgraduate Certificate in Applied Professional Studies (with or without endorsement) or in\n\r\ncognate subject for entry to the Postgraduate Diploma; or\n\r\n* A combination of academic and practical or professional experience based upon a record of\n\r\nlearning (academic transcript), parchment and a curriculum vitae detailing relevant\n\r\neducational achievements work experience and professional achievements as follows:\n\r\nEITHER\n\r\n* completion of a post-secondary school diploma in a related discipline at NZQA level 7 or\n\r\nequivalent from a recognised tertiary provider; and\n\r\n* demonstration of at least two years relevant practical experience with at least two written\n\r\nreferences (employer and character); or\n\r\n* demonstration of at least five years relevant practical or professional experience at a\n\r\nsenior level, evidence of ability to study at postgraduate qualification level and two written\n\r\nreferences (employer and character);\n\r\n* IELTS Academic 6.5 with no band score lower than 6.0, or equivalent or Waiariki English Test\n\r\n65 with no score lower than 60 for Mentoring and Leadership and Adult Teaching\n\r\nendorsements; or\n\r\n* IELTS Academic 7.0 with no band score lower than 7.0, or equivalent or Waiariki English Test\n\r\n70 with no score lower than 70 for Vulnerable Peoples, and Infection Prevention and Control\n\r\nendorsements.","strategicPurposeStatement":"The Postgraduate Certificate in Applied Professional Studies will enable learners to critically examine theories and models of continuing development through leadership. Students will have the opportunity to exhibit advanced knowledge and expertise in professional leadership in the context of their specialist field which compliments their current experience and/or qualifications. This qualification enables professionals in leadership roles to maintain currency and to critique their leadership practice.","graduateProfile":"Upon successful completion of the Postgraduate Certificate in Applied Professional Studies,\n\r\ngraduates will be able to:\n\r\n* Develop pedagogical content expertise within their field of study;\n\r\n* Critically evaluate their personal practice;\n\r\n* Critique challenges and issues within their specialist field and use to solve problems;\n\r\n* Operate within their communities of practice;\n\r\n* Advocate for self and others.","educationPathway":"Graduates of the Postgraduate Certificate in Applied Professional Studies will be eligible for entry\n\r\nto the Postgraduate Diploma in Applied Professional Studies programme or further Postgraduate\n\r\nstudy.","developedBy":"Toi Ohomai Institute of Technology","qualityAssuredBy":"New Zealand Qualifications Authority","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=3345"},"3355":{"i":"3355","entryRequirements":"Students must: * Be able to access a relevant workplace; and * Have a Bachelor degree or Graduate Certificate or Graduate Diploma in a cognate subject; or * Postgraduate Certificate in Applied Professional Studies (with or without endorsement) or in cognate subject for entry to the Postgraduate Diploma; or * A combination of academic and practical or professional experience based upon a record of learning (academic transcript), parchment and a curriculum vitae detailing relevant educational achievements work experience and professional achievements as follows: EITHER * completion of a post-secondary school diploma in a related discipline at NZQA level 7 or equivalent from a recognised tertiary provider; and * demonstration of at least two years relevant practical experience with at least two written references (employer and character); or * demonstration of at least five years relevant practical or professional experience at a senior level, evidence of ability to study at postgraduate qualification level and two written references (employer and character); * IELTS Academic 6.5 with no band score lower than 6.0, or equivalent or Waiariki English Test 65 with no score lower than 60 for Mentoring and Leadership and Adult Teaching endorsements; or * IELTS Academic 7.0 with no band score lower than 7.0, or equivalent or Waiariki English Test 70 with no score lower than 70 for Vulnerable Peoples, and Infection Prevention and Control endorsements.","strategicPurposeStatement":"The Postgraduate Certificate in Applied Professional Studies will enable learners to critically examine theories and models of continuing development through leadership. Students will have the opportunity to exhibit advanced knowledge and expertise in professional leadership in the context of their specialist field which compliments their current experience and/or qualifications. This qualification enables professionals in leadership roles to maintain currency and to critique their leadership practice.","graduateProfile":"Upon successful completion of the Postgraduate Certificate in Applied Professional Studies, graduates will be able to: * Develop pedagogical content expertise within their field of study; * Critically evaluate their personal practice; * Critique challenges and issues within their specialist field and use to solve problems; * Operate within their communities of practice; * Advocate for self and others.","educationPathway":"Graduates of the Postgraduate Certificate in Applied Professional Studies will be eligible for entry\n\r\nto the Postgraduate Diploma in Applied Professional Studies programme or further Postgraduate\n\r\nstudy.","developedBy":"Toi Ohomai Institute of Technology","qualityAssuredBy":"New Zealand Qualifications Authority","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=3355"},"3372":{"i":"3372","entryRequirements":"Entry Requirements: Students must: * Be able to access a relevant workplace; and * Have a Bachelor or Bachelor Honours degree in a cognate subject with a B average or above; or * Have a Postgraduate Certificate or Postgraduate Diploma in a cognate subject with a B average or above; or * In exceptional circumstances, based on academic merit and relevant experience, students who do not meet the academic and practical or professional experience requirements above may be considered for admission subject to the completion of any qualifying courses the Postgraduate Board of Studies may prescribe which must be completed either prior to or concurrently; and have * IELTS Academic 6.5 with no band score lower than 6.0, or equivalent or Waiariki English Test 65 with no score lower than 60 for Mentoring and Leadership and Adult Teaching endorsements; or * IELTS Academic 7.0 with no band score lower than 7.0, or equivalent or Waiariki English Test 70 with no score lower than 70 for Vulnerable Peoples, and Infection Prevention and Control endorsements.","strategicPurposeStatement":"The Master of Applied Professional Studies programme aims to provide a flexible range of study options that enable learners to match their needs to the qualification they are seeking to gain and to extend their domain of practice within a chosen professional field. The programme aims to integrate theory with practice within interrogative environments that support learners to broaden their knowledge and experience through: * Reflective practice * Critical evaluation * In-depth analysis of critical social, cultural and professional practice issues * Applied professional practice research * Synthesis and construction of new and/or innovative professional practice initiatives and/or applications * Articulation of personalised professional practice model(s) along with the theories, cultural understandings and experiences that inform the model(s)","graduateProfile":"Upon successful completion of the Master of Applied Professional Studies, graduates will be able to: * Perform as critically reflective and interdisciplinary practitioners; * Demonstrate leadership in their field of study nationally and internationally; * Analyse and synthesise information; * Use and apply knowledge to address novel or new problems and issues; * Develop and implement appropriate research methodologies; and * Exhibit higher level knowledge and understanding of their profession through cognitive, practical, personal and enabling skills.","educationPathway":"Graduates of the Master of Applied Professional Studies will be eligible for entry to other \n\r\nPostgraduate studies or a Doctoral degree.","developedBy":"Toi Ohomai Institute of Technology","qualityAssuredBy":"New Zealand Qualifications Authority","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=3372"},"3375":{"i":"3375","entryRequirements":"Entry Requirements: Students must: * Be able to access a relevant workplace; and * Have a Bachelor or Bachelor Honours degree in a cognate subject with a B average or above; or * Have a Postgraduate Certificate or Postgraduate Diploma in a cognate subject with a B average or above; or * In exceptional circumstances, based on academic merit and relevant experience, students who do not meet the academic and practical or professional experience requirements above may be considered for admission subject to the completion of any qualifying courses the Postgraduate Board of Studies may prescribe which must be completed either prior to or concurrently; and have * IELTS Academic 6.5 with no band score lower than 6.0, or equivalent or Waiariki English Test 65 with no score lower than 60 for Mentoring and Leadership and Adult Teaching endorsements; or * IELTS Academic 7.0 with no band score lower than 7.0, or equivalent or Waiariki English Test 70 with no score lower than 70 for Vulnerable Peoples, and Infection Prevention and Control endorsements.","strategicPurposeStatement":"The Master of Applied Professional Studies programme aims to provide a flexible range of study options that enable learners to match their needs to the qualification they are seeking to gain and to extend their domain of practice within a chosen professional field. The programme aims to integrate theory with practice within interrogative environments that support learners to broaden their knowledge and experience through: * Reflective practice * Critical evaluation * In-depth analysis of critical social, cultural and professional practice issues * Applied professional practice research * Synthesis and construction of new and/or innovative professional practice initiatives and/or applications * Articulation of personalised professional practice model(s) along with the theories, cultural understandings and experiences that inform the model(s)","graduateProfile":"Upon successful completion of the Master of Applied Professional Studies, graduates will be able to: * Perform as critically reflective and interdisciplinary practitioners; * Demonstrate leadership in their field of study nationally and internationally; * Analyse and synthesise information; * Use and apply knowledge to address novel or new problems and issues; * Develop and implement appropriate research methodologies; and * Exhibit higher level knowledge and understanding of their profession through cognitive, practical, personal and enabling skills.","educationPathway":"Graduates of the Master of Applied Professional Studies will be eligible for entry to other\n\r\nPostgraduate studies or a Doctoral degree.","developedBy":"Toi Ohomai Institute of Technology","qualityAssuredBy":"New Zealand Qualifications Authority","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=3375"},"3790":{"i":"3790","strategicPurposeStatement":"The Master of Contemporary Education (Level 9) aims to equip teachers and educational professionals with the competence, expertise, knowledge to drive and lead excellence in contemporary, future-focused and culturally responsive education practice. The qualification aims to build on and deepen capacity for and commitment to emerging technologies and digital fluencies within the education sector, in the bicultural context of Aotearoa New Zealand","graduateProfile":"Graduates of the Master of Contemporary Education will be able to lead education innovation and opportunities created through digital technologies and future-focused practices to advance education across New Zealand. They will be able to work collaboratively with students, parents, whanau, communities and other key stakeholders in a range of contexts to develop and adapt strategies to advance teaching practice and improve learner outcomes.   \n\r\nGraduates of the Master of Contemporary Education will be able to:   \n\r\nCritically engage in a substantial and authentic way with appropriate communities and stakeholders in the context of educational change.  \n\r\nCritique past, existing, and emerging practices and determine their cultural and social impact in the wider education context.   \n\r\nCritically analyse existing and emerging challenges and opportunities in contemporary education, and generate strategies to advance practice.  \n\r\nDevelop and adapt specialised knowledge regarding emerging and disruptive technologies, and the need for digital fluency, into practice.  \n\r\nLead and develop collaborative learnerfocused solutions in practice in the context of emerging challenges and opportunities in contemporary education.","educationPathway":"Professional doctorates in education from accredited tertiary institutions.","developedBy":"AcademyEx Education Limited Partnership","qualityAssuredBy":"New Zealand Qualifications Authority","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=3790"},"3920":{"i":"3920","entryRequirements":"Entry to the PGCEL programme may be limited by the number of places available. Applicants will normally be engaged in some form of educational practice.The academic entry requirement for the PGCELM is one of the following: * an NZQF-listed undergraduate degree (or equivalent); or * at the Dean's discretion, where an applicant's situation is compelling but not covered by the requirements listed above. Applicants from countries in which English is not the primary spoken language must demonstrate an acceptable level of English language fluency prior to acceptance in the programme. Ways in which English language fluency may be demonstrated include (i) completion of a programme of study in 11 HB4062 Postgraduate Certificate in Educational Leadership - Programme Document - Effective from 2019 which English was the language of instruction (conditions apply*), and (ii) approved scores on IELTS tests, namely an Academic score of 6.5 with no band lower than 6 (or accepted international equivalents).","strategicPurposeStatement":"The overall aim of the PGCEL programme is to enable learners to develop the advanced knowledge and skills to position themselves as leaders in their respective work places. Graduates of the PGCEL programme have sound understanding of theories of effective leadership and management and how to apply these in an educational setting. They will be prepared to manage relationships and resources in a manner that enhances learner achievement and workplace goals. Graduates will be able to reflect critically on their leadership practices in this regard, based on a sound rationale and philosophy.","graduateProfile":"Graduates of the PGCEL programme will be able to \n\r\n1. Critically evaluate and apply theories of and approaches to effective leadership and management, including culturally responsive pedagogy, in an educational setting. \n\r\n2. Build and sustain relationships that enhance learner achievement. \n\r\n3. Develop resource management skills that support the achievement of vision and goals for the educational setting. \n\r\n4. Critically evaluate their own leadership practices in relation to learner achievement. \n\r\n5. Articulate a philosophy and rationale for their approach to leadership and management in an educational setting.","educationPathway":"Further studies at Level 9 such as the Master of Professional Practice.  Pathway in through related Bachelor degree of Graduate Diploma.","developedBy":"Eastern Institute of Technology","qualityAssuredBy":"New Zealand Qualifications Authority","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=3920"},"3956":{"i":"3956","entryRequirements":"The minimum entry qualification for ECNZ's Master of Education (ECE) by thesis is a Bachelor Honours Degree or a Postgraduate Diploma. ECNZ's Master of Education (ECE) is open to those who have met the entrance requirements, including specified levels of attainment, in the programme admission regulations. Applicants from overseas must prove they hold an acceptable equivalent qualification from a recognised institution. They must also meet the English language proficiency requirements by achieving or exceeding 6.5 on the International English Language Testing System (IELTS) but with no band less than 6. Admission to the Master of Education (ECE) degree is based on the evaluation of documentary evidence (including the academic record) of the individual applicant's ability to undertake postgraduate study in a specialist field of enquiry or professional practice. For further information, refer to: https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/studying-in-new-zealand/understand-nz-quals/masters-degree/ Admission is at the discretion of ECNZ. The usual entry criterion is a grade average of at least a B. For ECNZ's postgraduate alumni, completion of their qualification provides evidence of ability to undertake a master's degree. Previous work experience may be considered.","strategicPurposeStatement":"The aim of this qualification is to: 1. Induct emerging researchers into the role of educational research for inquiry-based teaching and learning, who can demonstrate evidence of advanced knowledge in a specialist field of inquiry \n\r\n2. Prepare the next generation of researchers to undertake systematic and independent planning, execution, and completion of empirical research through culturally responsible and ethical research practice; this is achieved by partnering our students with knowledgeable supervisors and by establishing clear, effective research processes.  \n\r\n3. Grow a diverse community of scholars through research engagement that enables depth and breadth of research and researchers; this includes opportunities to study courses and conduct research in Early Childhood Leadership, Infants and Toddlers, Mātauranga Māori and the Montessori philosophy.  \n\r\n4. Develop and support the generation and dissemination of new, high quality, credible and meaningful research knowledge related to education in the Aotearoa New Zealand context and beyond. Co-publishing with supervisors is a fundamental practice for achieving this aim.","graduateProfile":"The Master of Education (ECE) Degree programme is underpinned by a set of learning outcomes that collectively encompass the requisite pedagogical and theoretical research knowledge for teacher researchers in educational settings. Overall, the expectation is that graduates of any master's programme should be able to: * show evidence of advanced knowledge about a specialist field of enquiry or professional practice; * demonstrate mastery of sophisticated theoretical subject matter; * evaluate critically the findings and discussions in the literature; * research, analyse and argue from evidence; * work independently and apply knowledge to new situations; and * engage in rigorous intellectual analysis, criticism and problem-solving. In addition, the learning outcomes of this proposed Master of Education (ECE) degree embed the specific aims of our programme as well as the essence of the conceptual framework and graduate profiles. The learning outcomes therefore reflect an expectation that students will engage critically with research complexity and understanding of the professional role of researchers and their responsibilities to staff, learners, programmes, service management, family and whanau.","educationPathway":"Upon completion of the M Ed. (ECE) graduates may wish to progress to doctoral study or to seek other related postgraduate qualifications. Both the coursework and thesis pathways provide opportunities for students to focus on ECE Leadership, Infants and Toddlers, Mātauranga Māori and from 2024 Montessori philosophy.","developedBy":"Te Rito Maioha Early Childhood New Zealand Incorporated","qualityAssuredBy":"New Zealand Qualifications Authority","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=3956"},"4194":{"i":"4194","entryRequirements":"Applicants must: * Provide evidence of competence in one of the languages of the national curriculum - English or Māori (acceptable evidence is outlined by the Teaching Council | Matatū Aotearoa at https://teachingcouncil.nz/content/language-competency-requirements) (TC Req 6.2) * Hold a Bachelor's degree at level 7 on the New Zealand Qualification Framework (NZQF), or recognised equivalent, with evidence of achievement at a B grade or above (TC Req 6.1) * Have a body of knowledge from NZQF Level 5 to 7 or higher in their entry qualification, that is relevant for teaching in a particular secondary school subject. Policy and procedure for assessment and administration of this is included in the recruitment-specific guidance (TC Req 6.1) * Prior to entry, provide evidence of literacy and numeracy at UE level or above OR successfully pass an assessment of literacy and numeracy with apassing grade no lower than the equivalent to UE in literacy and numeracy (TC Req 6.2) * Be assessed for their disposition to teach, prior to entry (this is completed as part of Selection) (TC Req 6.4) * Complete an assessment in Te Reo Māori, and make a commitment to developing their proficiency in Te Reo Māori throughout the programme (TC Req 6.3) * Complete a safety check, including (but not limited to) a New Zealand Police vet and an assessment of the risk, if any, that the applicant would pose to the safety of children if employed or engaged as a children's worker (under the Children's Act 2014 (previously Vulnerable Children Act 2014)) (TC Req 6.5) In addition to meeting the entry criteria, to be admitted to the programme applicants must meet the selection criteria. A candidate will be eligible to apply to the Teach First NZ Programme via the Rautahi Pathway if they: * has a depth of knowledge, experience and skills equivalent to an NZQF Level 7 qualification, and * has a body of knowledge at NZQF Level 5-7 in a New Zealand high school teaching subject which is in short teacher supply (e.g. Technology and Te Reo Māori), and * can provide satisfactory evidence of academic readiness to engage with and complete a Postgraduate Diploma","strategicPurposeStatement":"The aim of our work is to contribute to the development of an exceptional and high performing teacher and education leadership workforce which is supporting all young people to have fair opportunities and achieve success through education.   \n\n\n\r\nAs such, the educational outcomes we seek to achieve are to:  \n\r\n* Develop our students as deeply effective and transformational educators  \n\r\n* Increase our students' understanding of and commitment to addressing inequity and inequality, and working towards social justice in Aotearoa New Zealand   \n\r\n* Grow our students' leadership capability so that they can affect change within and beyond the classroom and school  \n\r\n* Ensure our students are able to practise in culturally sustaining ways for all people, as outlined in our tūāpapa  \n\n\n\r\nAs part of achieving these outcomes, our students will:  \n\r\nGain registered teacher status, by meeting the code and standards for the teaching profession 'with support', as outlined by the Teaching Council of New Zealand: Mātātu Aotearoa, and by undertaking Initial Teacher Education (ITE)","graduateProfile":"Graduate Profile:   \n\r\n(Outcome) A graduate of this programme:   \n\n\n\r\n* Will be able to use best practice theory and evidence to design and deliver learning opportunities and experiences which raise the achievement of all learners. They will reward risk-taking by their students, and will be steadfast in their rejection of deficit thinking and bad practice.   \n\n\n\r\n* Will be a critical and purposive agent for change, deeply committed to making a long-term difference in the lives of individuals and communities through education. They will create responsive and safe learning environments where learners are encouraged to take risks and to draw on their prior knowledge, experiences and beliefs to access, and sometimes re-define, success.  \n\n\n\r\n* Will understand the ongoing impact of widespread, systemic inequalities and have a particular commitment to social justice and equity in the service of individuals and communities unfairly made vulnerable.   \n\n\n\r\n* Will be rooted in teu le vā. This means that they will have a deep commitment to caring for and nurturing effective relationships with learners, colleagues and communities. In professional practice, this means continuously working to understand these relational spaces, to fostering ethical relationships with and between learners, and to acknowledging all shared experiences and stories that have passed between the stakeholders.  \n\n\n\r\n* Will be grounded in Mana Tangata. They will acknowledge their duty to re-indigenise education for their learners. They will give prominence to histories and stories which honour Māori and Polynesian ways of knowing and being. They will have a lived commitment to tangata whenua and Te Ao Māori and will be dedicated to an ongoing hīkoi towards a bi-cultural Aotearoa where all cultures are recognised, lived, valued and honoured, from day-to-day practice, to policy, governance and system change.","educationPathway":"Initial Teacher Education (ITE) Pathway, which leads to teacher registration with the Teaching Council | Matatū Aotearoa.","developedBy":"Teach First NZ Charitable Trust","qualityAssuredBy":"New Zealand Qualifications Authority","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=4194"},"4214":{"i":"4214","entryRequirements":"Applicants must: \n\r\n* Provide evidence of competence in one of the languages of the national curriculum - English or Māori (acceptable evidence is outlined by the Teaching Council | Matatū Aotearoa at https://teachingcouncil.nz/content/language-competency-requirements) (TC Req 6.2) \n\r\n* Hold a Bachelor's degree at level 7 on the New Zealand Qualification Framework (NZQF), or recognised equivalent, with evidence of achievement at a B grade or above (TC Req 6.1) \n\r\n* Have a body of knowledge from NZQF Level 5 to 7 or higher in their entry qualification, that is relevant for teaching in a particular secondary school subject. Policy and procedure for assessment and administration of this is included in the recruitment-specific guidance (TC Req 6.1) \n\r\n* Pass a literacy and numeracy assessment to assess competency, prior to entry, with the passing grade being no lower than the equivalent to UE in literacy and numeracy (TC Req 6.2) \n\r\n* Be assessed for their disposition to teach, prior to entry (this is completed as part of Selection) (TC Req 6.4) \n\r\n* Complete an assessment in Te Reo Māori, and make a commitment to developing their proficiency in Te Reo Māori throughout the programme (TC Req 6.3) \n\r\n* Complete a safety check, including (but not limited to) a New Zealand Police vet and an assessment of the risk, if any, that the applicant would pose to the safety of children if employed or engaged as a children's worker (under the Children's Act 2014 (previously Vulnerable Children Act 2014)) (TC Req 6.5) \n\r\nIn addition to meeting the entry criteria, to be admitted to the programme applicants must meet the selection criteria.","strategicPurposeStatement":"The aim of our work is to contribute to the development of an exceptional and high performing teacher and education leadership workforce which is supporting all young people to have fair opportunities and achieve success through education.   \n\n\n\r\nAs such, the educational outcomes we seek to achieve are to:  \n\r\n* Develop our students as deeply effective and transformational educators  \n\r\n* Increase our students' understanding of and commitment to addressing inequity and inequality, and working towards social justice in Aotearoa New Zealand   \n\r\n* Grow our students' leadership capability so that they can affect change within and beyond the classroom and school  \n\r\n* Ensure our students are able to practise in culturally sustaining ways for all people, as outlined in our tūāpapa  \n\n\n\r\nThis qualification is accredited at the successful completion of the first year of the Post Graduate Diploma in Secondary Teaching at the discretion of the Ako Mātātupu Academic Board.  \n\n\n\n\n\r\nThe Postgraduate Certificate in Secondary Education will not qualify a graduate to teach in Aotearoa New Zealand.","graduateProfile":"A graduate of this programme:   \n\n\n\r\n* Will understand how best practice theory and evidence should be used to design and deliver learning opportunities and experiences which raise the achievement of all learners. They will be steadfast in their rejection of deficit thinking and bad practice.   \n\n\n\r\n* Will be a critical and purposive agent for change, committed to making a long- term difference in the lives of individuals and communities. They will understand the importance of responsive and safe learning environments where learners are encouraged to take risks and to draw on their prior knowledge, experiences and beliefs to access, and sometimes re-define, success.  \n\n\n\r\n* Will understand the ongoing impact of widespread, systemic inequalities and have a particular commitment to social justice and equity in the service of individuals and communities unfairly made vulnerable.   \n\n\n\r\n* Will be rooted in teu le vā. This means that they will have a deep commitment to caring for and nurturing effective relationships with individuals, colleagues and communities.   \n\n\n\r\nWill be grounded in Mana Tangata. They will understand our duty as a nation to re-indigenise education for our tauira. They will have a lived commitment to tangata whenua and Te Ao Māori and will be dedicated to an ongoing hīkoi towards a bi-cultural Aotearoa where all cultures are recognised, lived, valued and honoured, from day to day practice, to policy, governance and system change.","educationPathway":"Further studies in eduction.","developedBy":"Teach First NZ Charitable Trust","qualityAssuredBy":"New Zealand Qualifications Authority","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=4214"},"4453":{"i":"4453","entryRequirements":"Applicants must hold an undergraduate degree or degree-equivalent, practical, professional or scholarly experience (e.g., technical writers, higher educators, graphic designers, librarians, scientists, corporate trainers, human resources, software developers, Kaiako Māori and others involved in training or education).  \n\n\n\r\nAdditional to the above, applicants will submit the following as part of their application for entry onto the programme:  \n\r\n- A structured resume or CV \n\n\n\r\nInternational students whose first language is not English must also demonstrate English language skills equivalent to an IELTS overall band score (Academic) of 6.5, with no band score less than 6.0, or equivalent, from one test taken in the preceding two years. \n\n\n\r\nSelection Criteria \n\r\nIn the case that there are more applicants than places available, candidates will be given preference based on a first come first served basis and on the below: \n\r\n- Within the past three years, having been employed in the training and development sector (e.g., government, education, or corporate sectors) and had a substantial role in designing or developing learning experiences. \n\r\n- Strength of the answered questions supplied during application and structured resume or CV.  \n\r\n- Interviews may be held where further information is required. \n\n\n\r\nThis will provide the best initial indicator of the applicant's level and experience in learning design and development and their potential aptitude for working within a learning design environment. \n\n\n\r\nWhere the number of successful applicants exceeds places available, a waiting list will be maintained, and students will be offered a position in waiting list order if one becomes vacant.","strategicPurposeStatement":"This programme provides both the education and training sectors with professionals who can critically apply and analyse learning theories, select appropriate learning technologies, and project manage in the context of instructional design. Graduates will integrate these skills and knowledge to design and develop engaging, quality learning experiences within corporate, government, and private sectors.  \n\n\n\r\nThe Postgraduate Certificate in Learning Design fills a gap that is present in learning design education in New Zealand and is targeted at a variety of learning and teaching professionals (e.g., technical writers, higher educators, graphic designers, librarians, scientists, corporate trainers, human resources, software developers and others involved in training or education).","graduateProfile":"The Postgraduate Certificate in Learning Design produces graduates who will be able to design and develop learning and training experiences in the corporate, government and education sectors. \n\n\n\r\nGraduates of this qualification will be able to: \n\r\n- Employ creative solutions to address learning design needs and goals while working to a client brief and/or industry needs. \n\r\n- Evaluate and apply learning approaches to design and develop learning experiences that meet the needs of multiple learning contexts. \n\r\n- Apply appropriate online learning design strategies and tools to create quality learning experiences. \n\r\n- Critically apply project management principles and tools to manage and engage stakeholders through a learning design project.","educationPathway":"Graduates of the programme may pathway into further postgraduate study in a related field.","developedBy":"Otago Polytechnic","qualityAssuredBy":"New Zealand Qualifications Authority","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=4453"},"4464":{"i":"4464","entryRequirements":"Entry to the Tohu Paerua o te Whakaako i te Mātauranga Kōhungahunga: Master of Teaching Early Childhood Education programme, including English language and academic requirements, will be in accordance with the criteria specified in the Toi Ohomai Regulatory Framework, and any additional criteria specified below. \n\r\nDetailed Requirements \n\r\n* Bachelor's degree at level 7 consisting of at least 360 credits*; or  \n\r\n* Bachelor Honours degree at level 8 consisting of at least 480 credits*; or  \n\r\n* Master's degree at level 9 consisting of at least 240 credits *; or  \n\r\n* A Teaching Council approved Diploma in Teaching (L7) (Primary or ECE)  \n\r\n* Candidates will be required to pass a literacy test. \n\r\n* Candidates will be required to pass a numeracy test.","strategicPurposeStatement":"This qualification has been designed to prepare teaching graduates with the skills to adapt to a changing education environment within the early childhood education sector. It has been designed to enhance and advance the knowledge and skills of early learning education practitioners in their professional careers. The qualification embraces and develops the student's current knowledge of te reo, tikanga and mātauranga Māori by engaging in authentic partnerships with tangata whenua and mana whenua in Aotearoa. It will raise the student's awareness, understanding and knowing of inclusion, multiculturalism, additional needs, refugees, new immigrants, age, gender and the anti-bias curriculum.  \n\n\n\r\nThis qualification is intentional and purposeful to advance and extend early childhood education teachers. Graduates from this qualification integrate theory with practice and practice with theory. They will investigate, research and explore challenges, issues of social justice in early childhood education settings and evaluate various solutions. All graduates will meet the requirements of the Graduating Teacher Standards: Aotearoa/New Zealand (Teaching Council of Aotearoa New Zealand, 2019).","graduateProfile":"1. Intentional Teachers - use their professional judgement to apply broad pedagogical knowledge and a deep understanding of a subject content and curricula to facilitate children's learning and development. \n\r\n2. Bicultural Teachers - implement a tikanga Māori approach to their work with learners, using Te Tiriti o Waitangi to guide their practice, drawing on the aspirations of whānau, hapū and iwi. \n\r\n3. Ethical Teachers - critically aware of their surroundings (including political) and know what to do and when to act. They are alert and responsive to the changes in their environment, using this knowledge to advocate for social justice.  \n\r\n4. Relational Teachers - skilled in communication and collaboration, and are aware and respectful of others. They have a deep understanding of their own identity and uphold the mana of all they work with.  \n\r\n5. Learning Teachers - demonstrate the disposition to be ongoing learners, committed to the sector and to their own personal and professional development. They are curious, creative and courageous beginning teachers. They are reflective, adaptive and work  \n\r\nready.  \n\r\n6. Sustainable Teachers - focus on meeting the needs of the present without compromising the needs of the future. They are conscious of sustaining the environment as well as their own well-being and that of others. \n\r\n7. Values-based Teachers - live and demonstrate the values of Toi Ohomai (manaakitanga, whanaungatanga, toitutanga and kotahitanga) in all their interactions with others.","educationPathway":"Graduates of the Tohu Paerua o te Whakaako i te Mātauranga Kōhungahunga: Master of Teaching Early Childhood Education will be eligible for entry to other Postgraduate studies or a Doctoral degree (Entry requirements of the provider Institution will apply).","developedBy":"Toi Ohomai Institute of Technology","qualityAssuredBy":"New Zealand Qualifications Authority","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=4464"},"4565":{"i":"4565","entryRequirements":"Applicants to the Postgraduate Diploma in Teaching (Secondary) must have qualified for the award of a Bachelor's degree at Level 7 on the New Zealand Qualifications Framework, or a recognised equivalent degree programme, and, through the advanced levels of their degree, show that they have a depth and breadth of knowledge allowing them lead learning in their chosen subject (or \n\r\nsubjects) at all levels in New Zealand secondary schools.","strategicPurposeStatement":"The purpose of the Postgraduate Diploma in Teaching (Secondary) is to support the New Zealand secondary school system by providing graduates who are job-ready on the first day of their teaching careers and able to teach students from Years 9 to 13 successfully with minimal induction or guidance. The programme will also ensure that graduates are able to operate programmes of learning support, which will assist graduates to support the learning aspirations of families within communities that may have lower learning outcomes - including Māori and Pasifika. The target group for this qualification includes those people who have already completed a degree that includes relevant and broad advanced study in a subject that is taught in the New Zealand secondary schools. Applicants must also be committed to teaching in New Zealand schools.","graduateProfile":"Graduates of the Postgraduate Diploma in Teaching (Secondary) will hold attitudes that would see them: \n\r\n* Exercise responsibility to cause learning \n\r\n* Problem-solve to ensure learning occurs for all learners \n\r\n* Act in the knowledge that all learners can learn \n\r\n* Act respectfully towards all learners \n\r\n* Act respectfully towards all cultures \n\r\n* Act respectfully towards parents \n\r\n* Act respectfully towards colleagues. \n\r\nGraduates of the Postgraduate Diploma in Teaching (Secondary) will be able to: \n\r\n1. Qualify for registration as a teacher in New Zealand, and practise professionally and ethically as a  \n\r\nteacher in the New Zealand school system \n\r\n2. Establish and manage safe and supportive learning environments in which school students are  \n\r\naffirmed as learners \n\r\n3. Manage the behaviour of school students, when necessary \n\r\n4. Identify school students' learning needs \n\r\n5. Plan learning activities that are based on identified learning needs \n\r\n6. Demonstrate knowledge of Te Mātaiaho content necessary for teaching school students from Years  \n\r\n9 to 13 \n\r\n7. Demonstrate knowledge of specialist curriculum subjects as taught in secondary schools \n\r\n8. Demonstrate knowledge of how school students learn \n\r\n9. Lead learning activities based on lesson plans \n\r\n10. Establish opportunities, when appropriate, for school students to have agency over their learning \n\r\n11. Identify learning caused through learning activities \n\r\n12. Incorporate tikanga Māori and te reo Māori into their professional work \n\r\n13. Reflect on their professional practice and engage in appropriate professional learning \n\r\n14. Operate as a supportive member of teaching teams \n\r\n15. Critique education literature to identify the truth in teaching. \n\n\n\r\nFurthermore, graduates of the Postgraduate Diploma in Teaching (Secondary) will hold these attitudes,  \n\r\ndemonstrate these skills and demonstrate the stated knowledge in diverse settings of the New Zealand  \n\r\nschool system involving children from Years 1 to 8, and children from across New Zealand's socio-economic  \n\r\nspectrum.","educationPathway":"Graduates will be ab le to apply for further postgraduate programmes of study.","developedBy":"New Zealand Graduate School of Education Limited","qualityAssuredBy":"New Zealand Qualifications Authority","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=4565"},"4566":{"i":"4566","entryRequirements":"Applicants to the Postgraduate Diploma in Teaching (Primary) must have qualified for the award of a Bachelor's degree at Level 7 on the New Zealand Qualifications Framework, or a recognised equivalent degree programme, and, through the advanced levels of their degree, show that they have a depth and breadth of knowledge allowing them lead learning in their chosen subject (or subjects) at all levels in New Zealand primary schools.","strategicPurposeStatement":"The purpose of the Postgraduate Diploma in Teaching (Primary) is to support the New Zealand primary school system by providing graduates who are job-ready on the first day of their teaching careers and able to teach students from Years 1 to 8 successfully with minimal induction or guidance. The programme will also ensure that graduates are able to operate programmes of learning support, which will assist graduates to support the learning aspirations of families within communities that may have lower learning outcomes - including Māori and Pasifika. The programme has a strong focus on the teaching of literacy and numeracy. The target group for this qualification includes those people who have already completed a degree, and are committed to teaching in New Zealand schools","graduateProfile":"Graduates of the Postgraduate Diploma in Teaching (Primary) will hold attitudes that would see them: \n\r\n* Exercise responsibility to cause learning \n\r\n* Problem-solve to ensure learning occurs for all learners \n\r\n* Act in the knowledge that all learners can learn \n\r\n* Act respectfully towards all learners \n\r\n* Act respectfully towards all cultures \n\r\n* Act respectfully towards parents \n\r\n* Act respectfully towards colleagues. \n\r\nGraduates of the Postgraduate Diploma in Teaching (Primary) will be able to: \n\r\n1. Qualify for registration as a teacher in New Zealand, and practise professionally and ethically as a teacher in the New Zealand school system \n\r\n2. Establish and manage safe and supportive learning environments in which school students are affirmed as learners \n\r\n3. Manage the behaviour of school students, when necessary \n\r\n4. Identify school students' learning needs \n\r\n5. Plan learning activities that are based on identified learning needs \n\r\n6. Demonstrate knowledge of Te Mātaiaho content necessary for teaching school students from Years 1 to 8 \n\r\n7. Cause learning in literacy and numeracy \n\r\n8. Demonstrate knowledge of how school students learn \n\r\n9. Lead learning activities based on lesson plans \n\r\n10. Cause learning activities in literacy and numeracy \n\r\n11. Establish opportunities, when appropriate, for school students to have agency over their learning \n\r\n12. Identify learning caused through learning activities \n\r\n13. Incorporate tikanga Māori and te reo Māori into their professional work \n\r\n14. Reflect on their professional practice and engage in appropriate professional learning \n\r\n15. Operate as a supportive member of teaching teams \n\r\n16. Critique education literature to identify the truth in teaching. \n\n\n\r\nFurthermore, graduates of the Postgraduate Diploma in Teaching (Primary) will hold these attitudes,  \n\r\ndemonstrate these skills and demonstrate the stated knowledge in diverse settings of the New Zealand  \n\r\nschool system involving children from Years 1 to 8, and children from across New Zealand's socio-economic  \n\r\nspectrum.","educationPathway":"Graduates will be able to apply for further postgraduate study.","developedBy":"New Zealand Graduate School of Education Limited","qualityAssuredBy":"New Zealand Qualifications Authority","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=4566"},"5170":{"i":"5170","entryRequirements":"1. Candidates for the Postgraduate Diploma in Professional Practice (Trauma-Responsive Therapy) shall before enrolment have:\n\r\na. Completed the BTI Postgraduate Certificate in Responding to Trauma OR an equivalent postgraduate diploma in a closely related field from another institution.\n\r\nb. Demonstrated a strong alignment with values of social change, justice, public good and people's well-being.\n\r\nc. Evidence of provisional or full membership of an appropriate professional body with supervised practice experience in responding to trauma.\n\r\nd. A letter of support from their supervisor/manager indicating that they are employed or volunteering in an existing vocational situation in the fields of mental health, counselling, psychology or other relevant profession which will provide a context for study and application of learning.\n\r\n2. Admission to the programme shall be granted or withheld upon consideration of these criteria together with a satisfactory selection interview focused specifically on professional context suitability, availability of time for study and access to appropriate technology and other resources.\n\r\nIn keeping with NZQA guidelines, where English is not a student's first language, or they did not undertake their undergraduate studies in English, they must demonstrate English language skills equivalent to an IELTS overall band score (academic) of 6.5, with no band score less than 6.0.","strategicPurposeStatement":"The Postgraduate Diploma in Professional Practice (Trauma-Responsive Therapy) extends skills, understandings and ways of being for biblically-inspired, culturally-attuned and research-informed therapeutic responses to clients experiencing trauma. With the growing awareness internationally of how adverse experiences can leave painful imprints of trauma across the lifespan and across our whānau and communities, the Postgraduate Diploma in Professional Practice (Trauma-Responsive Therapy) aims to enhance shalom - wellbeing and flourishing -in practitioners, their professional practice contexts and the clients they serve. The programme aims to meet the expressed needs of counsellors, psychotherapists, social workers, psychologists and other therapeutic professionals for in-depth understanding of trauma that integrates research-informed knowledge from many fields and for effective, ethical, evidence-based practice interventions and organisational responses.","graduateProfile":"Graduates will be able to: \n\r\n* Harmonise their professional, personal, cultural and spiritual identities to catalyse and sustain professional growth. \n\r\n* Engage with multiple perspectives with humility, empathy and grace. \n\r\n* Draw upon their faith and spirituality in a way that is a meaningful and integral part of their professional work \n\r\n* Apply evidence-based strategies in ethically aware and culturally-attuned trauma therapy","educationPathway":"Graduates will be able to progress to further postgraduate study.","developedBy":"Bethlehem Tertiary Institute","qualityAssuredBy":"New Zealand Qualifications Authority","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=5170"},"5171":{"i":"5171","entryRequirements":"1. Candidates for the Postgraduate Diploma in Professional Practice (Trauma-Informed Leadership \n\r\nand Service) shall before enrolment have:\n\r\na. Completed the BTI Postgraduate Certificate in Responding to Trauma OR an equivalent postgraduate diploma in a closely related field from another institution.\n\r\nb. Demonstrated a strong alignment with values of social change, justice, public good and people's well-being.\n\r\nc. A letter of support from their supervisor/manager indicating that they are employed or volunteering in an existing vocational situation which will provide a context for study and application of learning.\n\n\n\r\nIn keeping with NZQA guidelines, where English is not a student's first language, or they did not undertake their undergraduate studies in English, they must demonstrate English language skills equivalent to an IELTS overall band score (academic) of 6.5, with no band score less than 6.0.","strategicPurposeStatement":"The Postgraduate Diploma in Professional Practice (Trauma-Informed Leadership and Service) extends skills, understandings and ways of being for biblically-inspired, culturally-attuned and research-informed organisational responses to trauma. With the growing awareness internationally of how adverse experiences can leave painful imprints of trauma across the lifespan and across our whānau and communities, the Postgraduate Diploma in Professional Practice (Trauma-Informed Leadership and Service) aims to enhance shalom - wellbeing and flourishing - in practitioners and their professional practice contexts. It aims to meet the expressed needs of leaders in organisations interfacing with those experiencing ongoing trauma responses, professionals who desire to integrate research- based understandings of trauma and apply their learning in trauma-informed, effective, ethical, service and leadership practices leading to transformed professional contexts and more aware and compassionate communities.","graduateProfile":"Graduates will be able to: \n\r\n* Harmonise their professional, personal, cultural and spiritual identities to catalyse and sustain professional growth. \n\r\n* Engage with multiple perspectives with humility, empathy and grace. \n\r\n* Draw upon their faith and spirituality in a way that is a meaningful and integral part of their professional work \n\r\nPG Dip PP (Leadership) and (Trauma-Informed Leadership & Service) GPOs: \n\r\n* Critique the various benefits and challenges of different trauma-informed leadership approaches and locate themselves within this landscape. \n\r\n* Foster and facilitate trauma-responsiveness within organisational, community, whānau, hapū and iwi contexts","educationPathway":"Graduates will be able to progress to further postgraduate study.","developedBy":"Bethlehem Tertiary Institute","qualityAssuredBy":"New Zealand Qualifications Authority","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=5171"},"5201":{"i":"5201","entryRequirements":"Applicants must: \n\r\nProvide evidence of competence in one of the languages of the national curriculum - English or Māori (acceptable evidence is outlined by the Teaching Council | Matatū Aotearoa at https://teachingcouncil.nz/content/language-competency-requirements) (TC Req 6.2) \n\r\nHold a Bachelor's degree at level 7 (or above) on the New Zealand Qualification Framework (NZQF), or recognised equivalent (TC Req 6.1) \n\r\nProvide evidence of holding a body of knowledge at NZQCF Level 5 to 7 in the subject they are applying to train to teach in. Policy and procedure for assessment and administration of this is included in the relevant policy (TC Req 6.1) unless otherwise granted an exemption according to the approved \n\r\nTeachers' Institute Foundation Course (see below).\n\r\nPrior to entry, provide evidence of literacy and numeracy at UE level or above OR successfully pass an assessment of literacy and numeracy with a passing grade no lower than the equivalent to UE in literacy and numeracy (TC Req 6.2) \n\r\nBe assessed for their disposition and fitness to teach, prior to entry (TC Req 6.4) \n\r\nComplete an assessment in Te Reo Māori, and make a commitment to developing their proficency in Te Reo Māori throughout the programme (TC Req 6.3) \n\r\nComplete a safety check, including (but not limited to) a New Zealand Police vet and an assessment of the risk, if any, that the applicant would pose to the safety of children if employed or engaged as a children's worker (under the Children's Act 2014 (previously Vulnerable Children Act 2014)) (TC Req 6.5) \n\r\nBe accepted by a 'home school' within the TTI community which has attested that they are willing and able to support the candidate to complete the programme.\n\n\n\r\nIf an applicant indicates that they do not hold a Bachelor's Degree at \n\r\nNZQCF Level 7 but they meet all other entry criteria as outlined in these \n\r\nregulations, they will be assessed with all other candidates and will be \n\r\neligible to receive a conditional offer to enrol in the PGDip. At this stage, \n\r\ncandidates will be required to submit an audit of their prior experience \n\r\nmatched to the requirements for entry to the Foundation programme \n\r\nand the ITE programme they intend to enter, and to complete an \n\r\nassessment with a member of the TTI team. This assessment will \n\r\nconsider whether the candidate: \n\r\n? Has a combination of skills, experience and qualification(s) \n\r\nsuch that TTI is confident they have in-depth expertise in a \n\r\ncurriculum area that will enable them to advance student \n\r\nlearning; \n\r\n? Is likely to be able to successfully achieve the outcomes of \n\r\nthe Foundation programme and go on to study at a \n\r\npostgraduate level.  \n\r\nIf, in TTI's view, the candidate meets the above criteria, they will receive a \n\r\nconditional offer of enrolment. Amongst any other conditions of their \n\r\nenrolment, the candidate will be required to successfully complete the \n\r\nFoundation programme as outlined above prior to enrolment in the \n\r\nrelevant PGDip.","strategicPurposeStatement":"The postgraduate diploma in teaching (Secondary, School-Based) leads to teacher registration in Aotearoa New Zealand and aims to develop knowledgeable and skilful teachers who have the expertise to be effective and to continue to develop their practice.","graduateProfile":"The graduates of this programme will able to:  \n\r\n1. Apply the foundational knowledge and build the expertise required to engage in and undertake all aspects of being an effective teacher at the secondary school level.  \n\r\n2. Evaluate and adaptively apply a repertoire of practices and procedures that are underpinned by sound theoretical knowledge to meet the needs of secondary schools, classrooms and students.  \n\r\n3. Actively engage with research and undertake professional learning to build the ongoing specialist knowledge to continually improve, refine, and expand their teaching practice at the secondary school level.    \n\r\n4. Apply Te Tiriti o Waitangi, te reo Māori, and tikanga Māori in professional contexts as a secondary school teacher.  \n\r\n5. Enact a commitment to students, schools, education communities, and stakeholders as a secondary school teacher.  \n\r\n6. Critically apply and uphold the ethical and professional standards of teaching to advance the teaching profession.  \n\r\n7. Critically evaluate and apply knowledge, innovations and ideas from around the world in contexts of practice.","educationPathway":"Further study in education","developedBy":"The Teachers' Institute","qualityAssuredBy":"New Zealand Qualifications Authority","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=5201"},"5220":{"i":"5220","entryRequirements":"*\tAcademic Entry\n\r\nApplicants for the Master of Teaching and Learning (Early Childhood Education) must hold the minimum of a Level 7 degree, with a B+ average or higher.\n\n\n\r\n*\tSpecial Consideration\n\r\nSpecial consideration will be given to applicants who show evidence of successful prior academic performance and work experience that demonstrates an ability for postgraduate-level study. \n\n\n\r\nExamples of prior academic performance include, but are not restricted to:\n\r\no\tCompleted teaching qualification\n\r\no\tCompleted postgraduate qualification\n\r\nExamples of work experience includes, but not restricted to: \n\r\no\tExperience with young children, 0-5-year- olds (early childhood education) or 5-12-year-olds (primary/intermediate)   \n\r\no\tLeadership roles in community organisations that support young children  \n\r\no\tOwnership of, or management roles in early childhood organisations \n\n\n\r\nSpecial consideration entry is to ensure there is diversity of learners and there are no barriers in place that may restrict Māori, Pasifika, or disabled applicants, though the requirement of an undergraduate degree remains.\n\r\n*\tDemonstrate ability to meet the Teaching Council of Aotearoa New Zealand's Code of Professional Responsibility and the Standards for the Teaching Profession (2017).\n\r\n*\tAll students must meet the Teaching Council of Aotearoa New Zealand language requirements.\n\r\n*\tAll students must meet the literacy and numeracy requirements for the program, which is set at University Entrance. Students may meet the requirements through University Entrance NCEA literacy and numeracy standards or the TEC Literacy and Numeracy for Adults Assessment Tool.\n\r\n*\tInternational students must meet the NZQA Rule 22 language requirements.","strategicPurposeStatement":"The Master of Teaching and Learning program aims to cultivate teachers with a deep, critical understanding of the essential principles of early childhood education. This program will facilitate the development of expertise and skills vital for teaching, emphasizing the application of professional knowledge and skills to authentic examples of practice. Throughout the program, ākonga will engage in an advanced exploration of theory, pedagogy, and research in early childhood education. They will have opportunities to critically analyse current literature and research, refining their teaching practice to create innovative strategies that support the learning and development of tamariki. The program includes blocks of field practice placements, allowing ākonga to further hone their expertise and achieve comprehensive theoretical and practical outcomes in a range of early childhood settings in Aotearoa. Moreover, ākonga will be encouraged to pursue independent research and inquiry, enabling them to connect learning with practice. This approach enhances their ability to uphold and deliver high-quality programs to tamariki within the early childhood education sector. By the end of the program, graduates will be well-equipped to make meaningful contributions to and impact educational leadership among key stakeholders in early childhood settings.","graduateProfile":"1. Demonstrate a deep commitment to honouring Te Tiriti o Waitangi and a critical understanding of the unique status of tangata whenua in Aotearoa New Zealand. \n\r\n2. Critically employ advanced research and scholarship in early childhood education to develop and refine personal teaching philosophies and practices, addressing existing and emerging issues. \n\r\n3. Establish and maintain professional and ethical relationships with children, whānau, and communities, demonstrating leadership and collaborative expertise \n\r\n4. Foster and sustain a culture of inclusion, collaboration, and safety that ensures equitable outcomes for all learners, utilising advanced techniques and knowledge \n\r\n5. Design, implement, and critically assess children's learning across a range of New Zealand early childhood contexts, showcasing innovative and effective practices \n\r\n6. Demonstrate advanced pedagogical knowledge, including the implementation of effective play-based curricula, in diverse New Zealand early childhood contexts, taking responsibility for leadership in pedagogical practices","educationPathway":"Graduates of the Master of Teaching and Learning (Early Childhood Education) may be eligible to apply for a higher-level study. \n\n\n\r\nGraduation and graduates of the Master of Teaching and Learning (ECE) will not be guaranteed an entry into a Doctoral level programme and any entry will be subject to the rules and recognition by an enrolling institution.","developedBy":"New Zealand Tertiary College Limited","qualityAssuredBy":"New Zealand Qualifications Authority","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=5220"},"5428":{"i":"5428","entryRequirements":"Applicants must:\n\r\n-\tProvide evidence of competence in one of the languages of the national curriculum - English or Māori (acceptable evidence is outlined by the Teaching Council | Matatū Aotearoa at https://teachingcouncil.nz/content/language-competency-requirements) (TC Req 6.2)\n\r\n-\tHold a Bachelor's degree at level 7 on the New Zealand Qualification Framework (NZQF), or recognised equivalent (TC Req 6.1)\n\r\n-\tPrior to entry, successfully pass an assessment of literacy and numeracy with a passing grade no lower than the equivalent to UE in literacy and numeracy, with at least 14 numeracy credits at NCEA L2 or equivalent. (TC Req 6.2)\n\r\n-\tBe assessed for their disposition and fitness to teach, prior to entry (TC Req 6.4)\n\r\n-\tComplete an assessment in Te Reo Māori, and make a commitment to developing their proficiency in Te Reo Māori throughout the programme (TC Req 6.3)\n\r\n-\tComplete a safety check, including (but not limited to) a New Zealand Police vet and an assessment of the risk, if any, that the applicant would pose to the safety of children if employed or engaged as a children's worker (under the Children's Act 2014 (previously Vulnerable Children Act 2014)) (TC Req 6.5)\n\r\n-\tBe accepted by a 'home school' within the TTI community which has attested that they are willing and able to support the candidate to complete the programme.","strategicPurposeStatement":"The postgraduate diploma in teaching (Primary, School-Based) leads to teacher registration in Aotearoa New Zealand and aims to develop knowledgeable and skillful teachers who have the expertise to be effective and to continue to develop their practice. It is underpinned by the belief that in order to be able to teach effectively, trainees need to know both what to do and how to do it, but also why they are undertaking particular approaches and practices (Banks et al., 2024).  \n\n\n\r\nThe programme will be delivered in partnership with primary schools, primarily in Tāmaki Makaura | Auckland, with whom it has been developed, and who have sought to take a stronger lead in delivering a tailored, school-based initial teacher education programme which enables students to integrate theoretical learning about teaching with on-site (supervised) practice as a teacher. Students will primarily be recruited from the existing communities around each school, and many will be career-changers or mature students re-entering the workforce after a career break or returning from overseas. All students will be domestic.","graduateProfile":"The graduates of this programme will: \n\r\n1. Apply the foundational knowledge and build the expertise required to engage in and undertake all aspects of being an effective teacher at the primary school level, with a particular focus on literacy, English and mathematics. \n\r\n2. Evaluate and adaptively apply a repertoire of practices and procedures that are underpinned by sound theoretical knowledge, emphasising the science of learning, to meet the needs of primary schools, classrooms and students. \n\r\n3. Engage with research from both Aotearoa New Zealand and around the world, and undertake professional learning to build the ongoing specialist knowledge to continually improve, refine, and expand their teaching practice at the primary school level.   \n\r\n4. Apply Te Tiriti o Waitangi, te reo Māori, and tikanga Māori in professional contexts as a primary school teacher. \n\r\n5. Enact a commitment to students, schools, education communities, and stakeholders by upholding ethical and professional standards as a primary school teacher.","educationPathway":"Graduates of this programme will be able to proceed to further postgraduate study.","developedBy":"The Teachers' Institute","qualityAssuredBy":"New Zealand Qualifications Authority","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=5428"},"5440":{"i":"5440","entryRequirements":"Applicants need to be a minimum of 16 years old prior to starting the programme. International applicants must be a minimum of 18 years of age prior to starting the programme. * Applicants for the Postgraduate Diploma in Teaching (Secondary) must hold a bachelor's degree, at level 7 on the New Zealand Qualification and Credentials Framework (NZQCF) or a recognised equivalent degree programme. * Where an applicant's qualification has been undertaken outside of New Zealand, NZTC has discretion to determine equivalency to the New Zealand qualification. Equivalency is assessed on a case-by-case basis. * All applicants must: o Have sufficient depth and breadth of a subject relevant to the Aotearoa New Zealand secondary school curriculum from NZQCF Level 5 to 7 or higher in your entry qualification. Preference will be given for students applying to teach in subject specialist areas in short supply in New Zealand, such as the STEM, English, Learning Languages, and Social Science subjects. o Provide at least one professional reference. o Provide a statement of intent. o Demonstrate their ability to meet the expectations of the Teaching Council of Aotearoa New Zealand in terms of personal and professional qualities, based on the Code of Professional Responsibility and Standards for the Teaching Profession (2017), through a personal visual interview. o Take the Literacy and Numeracy for Adults Assessment Tool and attain Step 5 for literacy and Step 5 in numeracy where required. o Be assessed for their current te reo Māori competency based on the levels in Te Aho Arataki Marau and are expected to actively develop their te reo Māori knowledge throughout the programme. o Have attained a New Zealand Police vetting report. The report is uploaded to the New Zealand Police portal under the occupation \"Student Teacher\" to ensure the vetting includes the Children's Act 2014. If the report indicates the applicant has been convicted of a crime, the matter will be referred to Programme Board, which will decide on the entry of that applicant to the programme based on the applicant's response to the conviction, and support from an approved school. The school must be aware of the conviction. o Be physically and mentally fit to carry out the responsibilities of a teaching role safely and effectively. * International students must submit an overseas police certificate(s) for each overseas country they have lived in for more than one year within the last 10 years if they were 18 years old or over during the time they lived there. * Credit for prior formal study is awarded towards this qualification. *\tApplicants must provide evidence of meeting English language requirements according to the Teaching Council of Aotearoa New Zealand's English language proficiency requirements.\n\r\n*\tInternational applicants must also meet English language requirements according to Rule 22 of the New Zealand Qualifications Authority Programme Approval, Recognition, and Accreditation Rules 2025.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates of the PGDip(Sec) will build on prior undergraduate knowledge and skills, equipping student teachers with advanced competencies and practices that are necessary to be ready to teach upon graduation. Graduates will experience an immersion into pedagogy through their engagement in practice in school settings. There will be strong links between practice and theory as they engage deeply with theory and a wide range of in-depth pedagogies and assessment practices, as applied to their specialist Learning Areas in secondary schools. As part of reflective practice and fostering continuous improvement and professional growth, they will undertake cycles of inquiry to critically analyse contemporary approaches and practices as they refine their understanding and their own pedagogical practices. Graduates of this programme will emerge prepared to make a significant impact and contribution to the secondary sector in Aotearoa New Zealand.","graduateProfile":"The following graduate profile outcomes for this programme have been developed to align with The Standards and with stakeholder input to be specific to the PGDip(Sec): GPO1 Demonstrate commitment to honouring Te Tiriti o Waitangi and recognition of the unique status of tangata whenua in Aotearoa New Zealand. GPO2 Employ research and scholarship in secondary education to develop understanding about effective practices, especially related to your specialist learning area. GPO3 Establish and maintain professional and ethical relationships with rangatahi, whānau and communities. GPO4 Develop a culture of inclusion, collaboration and safety that provides equitable outcomes for all learners. Part B 3 (c) New Zealand Tertiary College GPO5 Design learning and assessment experiences for rangatahi in a range of New Zealand secondary contexts. GPO6 Demonstrate pedagogical content knowledge, including implementing effective technologies for teaching and learning experiences, in diverse New Zealand secondary contexts.","educationPathway":"Graduates may progress to relevant Master's programmes.","developedBy":"New Zealand Tertiary College Limited","qualityAssuredBy":"New Zealand Qualifications Authority","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=5440"},"113799":{"i":"113799","entryRequirements":"A Bachelor degree at Level 7 (360 credits) or higher qualification with an average grade deemed acceptable to the Academic Board or have completed the requirements for the Postgraduate Diploma in Education from NZTC with a grade point average of Merit or higher. Applicants for whom English is a second language and Te Reo Maori is not their first language must provide evidence of an Academic IELTS score issued within the last two years of at least 6.5 overall, 6.5 in writing with no band score lower than 6 or equivalent. IELTS scores may be combined from two tests as long as on one test a minimum of 6.5 overall and 6.0 in each component is achieved.","strategicPurposeStatement":"These qualifications aim to the support of professional development for early childhood leaders who have high levels of curriculum knowledge, research skills and capacity for leadership advancement. In additional the qualifications aim to engage students interested in advancing the academic, scholarship and research of the field of early childhood education.","graduateProfile":"Graduates will have acquired advanced professional knowledge and capabilities and investigated new developments in early childhood education. They will further have developed advanced research skills and scholarship in the field of early childhood education.","educationPathway":"Graduates of the Master of Education (Early Childhood Education) are eligible to apply for a doctoral programme in education at a university.","developedBy":"New Zealand Tertiary College Limited","qualityAssuredBy":"New Zealand Qualifications Authority","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=113799"},"113800":{"i":"113800","entryRequirements":"Applicants for the Postgraduate Diploma in Education must have a Bachelor degree at Level 7 (360 credits) or higher qualification. Applicants for whom English is a second language and Te Reo Maori is not their first language must provide evidence of an Academic IELTS score issued within the last two years of at least 6.5 overall, 6.5 in writing with no band score lower than 6 or equivalent. IELTS scores may be combined from two tests as long as on one test a minimum of 6.5 overall and 6.0 in each component is achieved.","strategicPurposeStatement":"These qualifications aim to the support of professional development for early childhood leaders who have high levels of curriculum knowledge, research skills and capacity for leadership advancement. In additional the qualifications aim to engage students interested in advancing the academic, scholarship and research of the field of early childhood education.","graduateProfile":"Graduates will have acquired advanced professional knowledge and capabilities and investigated new developments in early childhood education.","educationPathway":"Graduates of the Postgraduate Diploma in Education (Early Childhood Education) are able to pathway into a Master's program, such as the Master of Early Childhood Education or the Master of Education (Early Childhood Education).","developedBy":"New Zealand Tertiary College Limited","qualityAssuredBy":"Quality Assurance Division, NZQA","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=113800"},"VI001C":{"i":"VI001C","entryRequirements":"A bachelors degree and experience in middle or senior management in a secondary school.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates will be able to demonstrate understanding of educational leadership at the postgraduate level, enabling them to exercise professional leadership within a secondary school environment. They will be critically reflective and ethical leaders who resource and develop capacity to improve learning outcomes for a diverse range of students. They may continue to the Master of Secondary School Leadership (VI001B).","developedBy":"Victoria University of Wellington","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=VI001C"},"CN1904":{"i":"CN1904","entryRequirements":"An approved Bachelor's degree or equivalent.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates of the Postgraduate Diploma in Educational Psychology (PGDipEdPsyc) will be able to describe and apply key concepts, principles, and theories in educational psychology. They will have a strong working knowledge of learning sciences, assessment, behaviour, and inclusion, integrating insights from psychology and education. They will critically evaluate research and evidence-based practices, using scientific reasoning and methods to analyse and interpret educational data and phenomena.  Graduates will engage in integrative thinking and problem-solving, applying psychological principles to enhance teaching, learning, and student well-being. They will demonstrate ethical awareness in educational settings and incorporate bicultural confidence and competence, recognising their obligations under Te Tiriti o Waitangi. They will be able to communicate effectively in verbal and written formats across diverse professional and academic contexts, equipping them to contribute meaningfully to education, policy, and leadership in Aotearoa New Zealand and beyond.","graduateProfile":"A range of assessments will be used including structured reflective journals, case study analysis, design-based assessments, such as developing an intervention or instructional tool, critical essays and research critiques and recorded presentations or multimedia artefacts.","developedBy":"University of Canterbury","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=CN1904"},"AK1073":{"i":"AK1073","entryRequirements":"A bachelor's degree with a B grade average or higher in courses at level 7.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates of the MTchgLn in ECE and PgDipTchgLn in Primary will be equipped as beginning teachers with the knowledge, skills and dispositions to teach diverse learners in New Zealand classrooms and Early Learning Services. They will be critically reflexive practitioners with adaptive expertise expressed through responsive practice for enhanced student learning outcomes. Such graduates will meet the New Zealand Teaching Council Code and Standards and will qualify for provisional registration in New Zealand.","developedBy":"Auckland University of Technology","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=AK1073"},"AU5647":{"i":"AU5647","entryRequirements":"A relevant Bachelors degree or an Advanced Diploma in Teaching or a professional qualification in teaching with at least two years of prior professional experience.","strategicPurposeStatement":"PGDipTLDL graduates will understand the principles of Second Language Acquisition and Bilingual/Plurilingual Education and be able to enhance the learning, capabilities and outcomes of all learners, in particular those from diverse language backgrounds.","developedBy":"University of Auckland","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=AU5647"},"MY1075":{"i":"MY1075","entryRequirements":"A New Zealand university degree, or equivalent qualification and/or relevant professional experience.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates will have a strong appreciation of specialist teaching as an interdisciplinary field of practice. They will also have a thorough understanding of how to apply their specialist teaching knowledge and skills in diverse cultural contexts.","developedBy":"Massey University","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=MY1075"},"CN1096":{"i":"CN1096","entryRequirements":"An approved Bachelor's degree.","strategicPurposeStatement":"A graduate of the MEd (Thesis - 120 points) will be able to demonstrate advanced knowledge in their field of study, a mastery of sophisticated theoretical subject matter, the ability to evaluate critically the findings and discussions in the literature, to research, analyse and argue from evidence.  This includes the experience of completing a substantial piece of original research.","developedBy":"University of Canterbury","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=CN1096"},"AK1036":{"i":"AK1036","entryRequirements":"Bachelor's degree or equivalent. In exceptional circumstances an appropriate combination of academic and professional experience deemed equivalent to a bachelor's degree may be considered.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates of the Postgraduate Diploma in Higher Education will demonstrate an advanced knowledge and critical reflection on their practice, which prepares them for further research in the field of teaching and learning, as well as further postgraduate study.","developedBy":"Auckland University of Technology","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=AK1036"},"VI001L":{"i":"VI001L","entryRequirements":"A bachelors degree with a major in a recognised secondary school teaching subject with a grade point average of at least a B in the final year of study. Preference may be given to applicants with qualifications in science, mathematics or te reo Mâori.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates will have advanced knowledge of the professional practice of evidence based teaching and curriculum implementation in the secondary school context. They will have the skills and knowledge to establish and sustain a positive, inclusive learning environment and will be able to evaluate their effectiveness using evidence of student learning progress. They will be able to critique research literature, teaching practices, and educational policy and will use their knowledge to develop the learning potential of all of their students. Graduates will be eligible to apply for teacher registration in New Zealand or progress to doctoral study with additional research methods study","developedBy":"Victoria University of Wellington","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=VI001L"},"AU5539":{"i":"AU5539","entryRequirements":"An approved degree with a minimum GPA, or an equivalent qualification; and courses at stages III or IV in a teaching subject; and the potential to meet the criteria for provisional registration with the Education Council of Aotearoa New Zealand.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates of the MTchg(Secondary) will possess the knowledge, skills and dispositions to make a positive difference for learners from priority student groups. They will be critical, self-motivated, resilient and collaborative practitioners. Graduates will have a critical understanding of how to conduct an investigation and use research and evidence to inform and develop their professional knowledge and practice","developedBy":"University of Auckland","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=AU5539"},"AK1047":{"i":"AK1047","entryRequirements":"A bachelors degree.","strategicPurposeStatement":"They will be critically reflexive practitioners with adaptive expertise expressed through responsive practice for enhanced student learning outcomes. Such graduates will meet the New Zealand Teachers Council Graduating Teacher Standards and will qualify for provisional registration in New Zealand.","developedBy":"Auckland University of Technology","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=AK1047"},"MY0168":{"i":"MY0168","entryRequirements":"Master's degree in Education, Psychology, Counselling, Social Work, Speech Language Therapy, or related relevant discipline, having achieved a grade average of at least a B; 60 credits of approved undergraduate psychology; and relevant professional experience.","strategicPurposeStatement":"The graduates will know and understand the theoretical underpinnings of practice in educational psychology. They will demonstrate ability to apply a broad range of psychological knowledge in practice and to carry out research in this field.","developedBy":"Massey University","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=MY0168"},"AK1059":{"i":"AK1059","entryRequirements":"A bachelor's degree or equivalent.","strategicPurposeStatement":"PgCertEdPrac graduates will demonstrate an advanced knowledge and level of reflection on their practice, which prepares them for further postgraduate study.","developedBy":"Auckland University of Technology","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=AK1059"},"VI003Z":{"i":"VI003Z","entryRequirements":"A bachelor's degree in a relevant subject area with an average of B or better along with at least two years of related professional experience.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates of the PGCertTESOL will demonstrate advanced knowledge of core topics in the teaching of second languages, the nature of language and the nature of second language learning. They will be able to engage with the relevant literature critically and creatively and will be able to apply this knowledge effectively to teaching practice and to communication about language teaching and learning. Graduates from the PGCertTESOL may be able to continue to the PGDipTESOL and to the MA in TESOL.","developedBy":"Victoria University of Wellington","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=VI003Z"},"OO0535":{"i":"OO0535","entryRequirements":"A bachelor's degree and registration to teach in New Zealand primary and/or secondary schools or equivalent, and teaching experience.","strategicPurposeStatement":"This postgraduate certificate is for professionals already working in the teaching and/or education sector. Completing this qualification will enable teachers to compete with new graduates and to show their advanced knowledge in their chosen profession.","developedBy":"University of Otago","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=OO0535"},"VI00Y7":{"i":"VI00Y7","entryRequirements":"A bachelors degree with a major in education and/or psychology, or an approved teacher education degree with an average grade of B or higher.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates will have the knowledge and skills to effectively support learners, their families and educational personnel. They will advance their careers in education-related contexts as leaders utilising contemporary evidence-based practices. They will become leaders in educational centres, as in-service teacher-educators or will work in education-related policy. The Master of Educational Psychology can be either a stand-alone degree but is also a prerequisite for the internship-year qualification, the Postgraduate Diploma in Education Psychology Practice (VI00Z2), which leads to registration as a psychologist. Alternatively, some graduates may be able to continue to a PhD.","developedBy":"Victoria University of Wellington","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=VI00Y7"},"WI0132":{"i":"WI0132","entryRequirements":"Bachelor of Teaching or equivalent","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates are able to provide a systematic and coherent account of the knowledge, ideas, principles, concepts, chief research methods, and problem-solving techniques in the field of education.","developedBy":"University of Waikato","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=WI0132"},"CN0938":{"i":"CN0938","entryRequirements":"An approved Bachelor's degree","strategicPurposeStatement":"The Hoaka Pounamu | Maori Bilingual and Immersion Teaching and Learning programme aims to develop high-quality graduates who will promote leadership and raise the knowledge of te reo Maori in bilingual, immersion, and mainstream settings. Hoaka Pounamu | Maori Bilingual and Immersion Teaching and Learning is designed to extend teachers on the use of te reo Maori in the classroom.","developedBy":"University of Canterbury","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=CN0938"},"AC1097":{"i":"AC1097","entryRequirements":"A Bachelor of Education (Teaching) or approved equivalent qualification","strategicPurposeStatement":"BEd(Tchg)(Hons) graduates will have an advanced knowledge and understanding of the principles, concepts and ideas that define and inform teaching. They will be critical, reflective practitioners able to pursue personal professional goals and postgraduate study.","graduateProfile":"Formative feedback aims at enhancing a candidates prof development and competence in line with module outcomes.  Summative feedback establishes a candidate's grade for each module.","developedBy":"University of Auckland","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=AC1097"},"WI0807":{"i":"WI0807","entryRequirements":"Postgraduate Diploma in Education (Special Education) or equivalent.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates will have advanced understanding and knowledge about the specialist body of theoretical and applied topics relevant to special education.They will understand the underlying concepts and principles associated with special education, the main methods of enquiry in special education and the different approaches to solving problems in special education.","developedBy":"University of Waikato","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=WI0807"},"MY1076":{"i":"MY1076","entryRequirements":"A New Zealand university degree, or equivalent qualification and/or relevant professional experience.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates will have a strong appreciation of specialist teaching as an interdisciplinary field of practice, and in-depth knowledge and skills in their endorsed area of specialist teaching. They will also have a thorough understanding of how to apply their specialist teaching knowledge and skills in diverse cultural contexts.","graduateProfile":"*","educationPathway":"*","developedBy":"Massey University","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=MY1076"},"OO0534":{"i":"OO0534","entryRequirements":"A bachelor's degree and registration to teach in New Zealand primary and/or secondary schools or equivalent, and teaching experience.","strategicPurposeStatement":"This postgraduate diploma is for professionals already working in the teaching and/or education sector. Completing this qualification will enable teachers to compete with new graduates and to show their advanced knowledge in their chosen profession.","developedBy":"University of Otago","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=OO0534"},"AK1053":{"i":"AK1053","entryRequirements":"Applicants must have completed the following or equivalent: the Bachelor of Education [Specialty] Teaching or the Graduate Diploma in Teaching with a B grade average or higher in papers at level 7 or equivalent.","strategicPurposeStatement":"MEd graduates will have a broad range of research skills for application to their area of education. They will have sound analytical and critical thinking skills and be able to develop, apply and test advanced theoretical concepts in education. They will have presented the results by way of a dissertation or thesis. They may progress to further study at doctoral level.","developedBy":"Auckland University of Technology","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=AK1053"},"MY0362":{"i":"MY0362","entryRequirements":"Bachelor's degree in Education, Psychology, Counselling, Social Work, Speech Language Therapy, or a related relevant discipline, having achieved a grade average of at least a B, or equivalent; and 60 credits of approved undergraduate psychology.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates of the Master of Educational and Developmental Psychology will be able to effectively and successfully demonstrate skills in the integration and application of educational, developmental, and psychological discipline knowledge and research to support the lifelong learning and development of 21st century citizens. They will be able to contribute to the research and scholarship that guides and informs professional practice and the wider discipline. Graduates are distinguished by their ability to critically apply discipline knowledge and research, to better understand the complex dimensions of a range of current educational, developmental and psychological issues, practices and/or policies.     Graduates will possess the necessary foundations to pursue further postgraduate study for professional practice in Psychology, Educational and Developmental Psychology, Education and related fields.  Graduates will develop skills and experience that will enhance and advance their careers in the following areas:  * Inclusive and special education (early childhood to tertiary)  * Professional development and human management within organisations   * Leadership and consultation in educational settings  * Behaviour and learning support work and specialist education support  * Community support and consultation roles  * Research and policy development","graduateProfile":"NA","educationPathway":"NA","developedBy":"Massey University","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=MY0362"},"AU5159":{"i":"AU5159","entryRequirements":"Bachelor of Education (Teaching) or equivalent qualification recognised for teacher registration in New Zealand.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Postgraduate Diploma in Education (Teaching) graduates will have built on their previous teachers professional knowledge and practice. They will be equipped with research skills and knowledge that will provide a gateway to further study at the postgraduate level, and improve their current classroom/centre professional practice.","developedBy":"University of Auckland","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=AU5159"},"AU5517":{"i":"AU5517","entryRequirements":"Admission on the basis of achievement in the first three years of the Bachelor of Physical Education (normally a B average).","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates of the Bachelor of Physical Education (Honours) programme will know and understand the principles, concepts and ideas that underpin the field of Health and Physical Education. They will be socially critical professionals who will be qualified to pursue careers in teaching and/or further postgraduate study.","developedBy":"University of Auckland","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=AU5517"},"VI005C":{"i":"VI005C","entryRequirements":"The entry requirements for the MTESOL are a Bachelor's degree (or equivalent qualification) with a grade average of B or better in Linguistics, English Language, Education or a language other than English (or another relevant degree), and at least two years of professional experience in language education or a related field. Applicants without relevant experience will be considered on a case-by-case basis and may be expected to complete bridging courses depending on the nature and extent of their previous experience and qualifications.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates of the MTESOL will demonstrate advanced knowledge of a range of topics on the nature of language, second language learning and the teaching of English as an additional language. They will be able to engage critically and creatively with the relevant research literature and apply knowledge from research to the teaching and learning of additional languages and to communication about language teaching and learning. The programme develops high-level skills for work in language and education policy and prepares students for entry into PhD programmes in New Zealand or internationally.","developedBy":"Victoria University of Wellington","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=VI005C"},"AU5603":{"i":"AU5603","entryRequirements":"Have completed the requirements for an approved degree, and b) be currently employed in the tertiary education sector with a substantial role in teaching or supporting student learning, or have been employed in such a role within the past three years.","strategicPurposeStatement":"PGDipHigherEd graduates will be equipped to teach and research their teaching in a way that enhances the learning, capabilities and graduate outcomes of all their students; to innovate and exhibit leadership in their teaching; and to foster a community of practice that enables them to develop and share their practice with colleagues and enhance the standard and standing of teaching in their faculty.","developedBy":"University of Auckland","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=AU5603"},"CN1057":{"i":"CN1057","entryRequirements":"A New Zealand teacher education degree, or a recognised three year diploma in early childhood, primary or secondary teaching or equivalent qualification and/or relevant professional experience.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates will have a strong appreciation of specialist teaching as an interdisciplinary field of practice. They will also have a thorough understanding of how to apply their specialist teaching knowledge and skills in diverse cultural contexts.","developedBy":"University of Canterbury","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=CN1057"},"WI0915":{"i":"WI0915","entryRequirements":"A bachelors degree, bachelors with honours degree or equivalent, or a postgraduate diploma in a subject relevant to the Master of Teaching and Learning.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates of the Master of Teaching and Learning will be equipped as beginning teachers with the knowledge, skills and dispositions to teach diverse learners effectively in New Zealand schools and meet the New Zealand Teachers Council Graduating Teacher Standards. They will have a critical understanding of research based pedagogical approaches, learning theory and content knowledge to develop and use effective high leverage teaching practices. They will expect to engage in a continuous cycle of questioning, observing, acting, and learning. They will understand the nature of adaptive and responsive evidence-based practice to support learning for all students. In short, they will be competent, independent and responsible beginning professionals who will be highly sought after by schools.","developedBy":"University of Waikato","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=WI0915"},"AU5505":{"i":"AU5505","entryRequirements":"A Postgraduate Diploma in Educational Leadership, a professional qualification in teaching and at least three years practical experience in teaching or in a related profession, including experience in a formal or informal leadership and/or management role","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates of the MEdLd will have knowledge, skills, and experience in the field of educational leadership. They will be critical, reflective leaders who can make a positive difference to learning outcomes for students and teachers across education sectors. They will have developed the research capabilities to undertake independent research in the field of educational leadership.","developedBy":"University of Auckland","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=AU5505"},"MY0438":{"i":"MY0438","entryRequirements":"A New Zealand university degree, or equivalent qualification and/or relevant professional experience.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates will have a strong knowledge and appreciation of specialist teaching as an interdisciplinary field of practice, and in-depth knowledge and skills in their endorsed area of specialist teaching. They will have a thorough understanding of how to apply their specialist teaching knowledge and skills in diverse cultural contexts. They will also be able to undertake research within their area of specialist teaching and inclusive education.","developedBy":"Massey University","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=MY0438"},"CN0914":{"i":"CN0914","entryRequirements":"A bachelor's degree including two final-year courses in education;or any bachelor's degree plus a one-year teaching qualification;or any bachelor's degree plus teaching or counselling experience in a recognised educational or counselling context.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates will have an advanced research-based understanding and knowledge of contemporary educational theory and professional practice. The Diploma emphasises critical thinking about and understanding of issues across a range of educational areas.","developedBy":"University of Canterbury","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=CN0914"},"CN1014":{"i":"CN1014","entryRequirements":"An approved Bachelor's degree","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates of the PGDipYCL will be able to:  (a) critically evaluate the suitability of diverse forms of leadership in relation to their personal professional context,  (b) understand and navigate the complexities of community engagement and service learning,  (c) evaluate a local youth or community situation in light of global social challenges as addressed in the UN Sustainable Development Goals,  (d) consider diverse cultural approaches to leadership, youth and community through the lens of matauranga Maori,  (e) engage with people and their communities with confidence and competence with consideration of Te Tiriti o Waitangi articles.  (f) gain advanced knowledge and understanding in a focus area of study.","developedBy":"University of Canterbury","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=CN1014"},"AK1054":{"i":"AK1054","entryRequirements":"Bachelor's degree or equivalent qualification with a B grade average or higher in papers at level 7 or equivalent plus at least 2 years relevant professional experience.","strategicPurposeStatement":"MEdPrac graduates will be reflective practitioners with the knowledge and capabilities to critically analyse, reflect on and modify effectively their own educational practice; independently assess and critique new information and research relevant to their chosen area of interest in education practice; and, work effectively and collaboratively with  people from diverse cultural backgrounds.","developedBy":"Auckland University of Technology","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=AK1054"},"AK1287":{"i":"AK1287","entryRequirements":"A bachelor's degree and either a year of advanced study in a relevant field or professional and /or scholarly experience, or equivalent.","strategicPurposeStatement":"MPLS graduates will possess advanced academic knowledge and analytical capabilities in language studies and be able to apply such advanced academic knowledge/capabilities rigorously, analytically and creatively to new situations and problems. They will critically analyse, reflect on and modify effectively their own practice; independently assess and critique new information and research relevant to language studies; show mastery of the reflective practice process in order to work independently and manage their ongoing professional development; work expertly, effectively and collaboratively with people from diverse cultural backgrounds.","developedBy":"Auckland University of Technology","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=AK1287"},"AU5791":{"i":"AU5791","developedBy":"University of Auckland","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=AU5791"},"CN0945":{"i":"CN0945","entryRequirements":"An approved Bachelor's degree.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates will have advanced knowledge and integrated understanding  of contemporary educational theory and practice. They will be able to engage in intellectual analysis, criticism and problem-solving and be highly knowledgeable teachers who are innovative, adaptable and resilient in supporting and enhancing the diverse learning strengths of each of their students in ways that result in positive educational outcomes. They will be committed to, and skilful in, developing and sustaining partnerships with family, whanau, hapu, iwi, aiga, communities and other educational and health professionals.","developedBy":"University of Canterbury","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=CN0945"},"AK3744":{"i":"AK3744","entryRequirements":"A bachelor's degree or equivalent.","strategicPurposeStatement":"PgCertEd graduates will demonstrate an advanced level of understanding of issues in a selected area of study in education. They will be competent and reflective practitioners and be prepared for further postgraduate study.","developedBy":"Auckland University of Technology","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=AK3744"},"OO0497":{"i":"OO0497","entryRequirements":"A bachelor's degree with an average grade of at least B+ in the final year of study, or equivalent.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates of this degree programme will have met the requirements for provisional teacher registration in New Zealand.","graduateProfile":"1. Communication\n\r\nThe ability to communicate information, arguments and analyses effectively and appropriately to colleagues and learners as appropriate to the academic and professional settings in which they operate.\n\n\n\r\n2.Critical Thinking\n\r\nThe ability to analyse issues logically, consider different options and viewpoints, and make informed decisions which will guide their teaching and further their professional growth.\n\n\n\r\n3.Cultural Understanding\n\r\nAn understanding of cultural issues and cultural diversity in New Zealand within the bicultural framework of the Treaty of Waitangi; knowledge and appreciation of multiculturalism and an ability to incorporate such understandings in their professional philosophy and practice.\n\n\n\r\n4. Ethics\n\r\nA knowledge of ethics, and commitment to ethical standards of the teaching profession and recognition of their social responsibility as teachers.\n\n\n\r\n5. Global Perspective\n\r\nAn appreciation of the global perspectives in teaching and an informed sense of international issues in education.\n\n\n\r\n6. Environmental Literacy\n\r\nCommitment to environmental literacy, care of the whenua and sustainability.\n\n\n\r\n7. Numeracy and Literacy\n\r\nKnowledge and skills related to teaching of literacy and numeracy to learners in their professional settings.\n\r\n8. In-depth Knowledge\n\r\nA deep, coherent and extensive knowledge of learners, curriculum, learning and teaching, assessment, transitions and subject area(s) applicable to learners in their professional settings based on an understanding and application of the fundamental contributions of research to teaching and learning.\n\n\n\r\n9. Information Literacy\n\r\nAn understanding of information literacy and specific skills in acquiring, organising and presenting information, in particular through computer-based activity, using these in teaching.\n\n\n\r\n10. Interdisciplinary Perspective\n\r\nIntellectual openness and curiosity; awareness of the limits of current knowledge and of the interdisciplinary links between curriculum areas in approaches to teaching and learning in professional settings.\n\n\n\r\n11. Professional Commitment to social justice and excellence in teaching and learning for the inclusion of all learners for whom they are responsible in their professional setting.","developedBy":"University of Otago","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=OO0497"},"AU5137":{"i":"AU5137","entryRequirements":"BA (Honours) or equivalent in a relevant subject; OR a degree of Bachelor of Arts with a major in a relevant subject and a DipTESOL. See Calendar for full details.","strategicPurposeStatement":"to give graduates a strong academic grounding in the teaching and learning of English as a second or foreign language","developedBy":"University of Auckland","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=AU5137"},"MY0623":{"i":"MY0623","entryRequirements":"Relevant bachelor's degree with honours, or masterate degree or equivalent.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates will have both the level of knowledge and understanding and capability for ongoing learning and inquiry, enabling them to function as leaders in a broad range of professional education settings.","developedBy":"Massey University","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=MY0623"},"AU4055":{"i":"AU4055","entryRequirements":"Completed the requirements for a Masters degree in a relevant subject, or an approved equivalent qualification, with a minimum GPA and has the training and ability to pursue advanced doctoral study in fine arts creative practice and research.","strategicPurposeStatement":"EdD graduates are able to make an advanced, original contribution to research in educational professional practice to an internationally recognized standard, demonstrate knowledge of literature relevant to the subject or field, and exercise critical and analytical judgment of it.","developedBy":"University of Auckland","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=AU4055"},"AK3743":{"i":"AK3743","entryRequirements":"A bachelor's degree in the subject to be taken for the Postgraduate Diploma, or equivalent.","strategicPurposeStatement":"PgDipEd graduates will have advanced knowledge and skills in their chosen area of education essential for them to be able to pursue personal and professional goals and further postgraduate study. The emphasis placed on integrating theory and practice will enhance problem solving and analytical skills developed at undergraduate level.","developedBy":"Auckland University of Technology","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=AK3743"},"CN0976":{"i":"CN0976","entryRequirements":"An approved Bachelor's degree.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates will have a strong appreciation of specialist teaching as an interdisciplinary field of practice and in-depth knowledge and skills in their endorsed area of specialist teaching. They will also have a thorough understanding of how to apply their specialist teaching knowledge and skills in diverse cultural contexts and the ability to plan, implement, mentor and evaluate research-informed strategies within their professional context.","developedBy":"University of Canterbury","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=CN0976"},"CN0965":{"i":"CN0965","entryRequirements":"An approved Bachelor's degree.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates will have advanced knowledge and integrated understandings and experience of contemporary educational theory and professional practice. They will be able to engage in intellectual analysis, criticism and problem-solving in relation to practice challenges.  This will enable them to be highly knowledgeable teachers who are innovative, adaptable and resilient in supporting and enhancing the diverse learning strengths of each of their students in ways that result in positive educational outcomes. They will be committed to, and skilful in, developing and sustaining partnerships with family, whanau, hapu, iwi, aiga, communities and other educational and health professionals.","developedBy":"University of Canterbury","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=CN0965"},"CN1015":{"i":"CN1015","entryRequirements":"An approved Bachelor's degree.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates of the PGCertYCL will be able to:  (a) critically evaluate the suitability of diverse forms of leadership in relation to their personal professional context,  (b) understand and navigate the complexities of community engagement and service learning,  (c) evaluate a local youth or community situation in light of global social challenges as addressed in the UN Sustainable Development Goals,  (d) consider diverse cultural approaches to leadership, youth and community through the lens of matauranga Maori,  (e) engage with people and their communities with confidence and competence with consideration of Te Tiriti o Waitangi articles.","developedBy":"University of Canterbury","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=CN1015"},"VI001U":{"i":"VI001U","entryRequirements":"A bachelors degree with a major in education, psychology or a related discipline, or an approved teacher education degree, or a bachelors degree and a teaching diploma from an approved teacher education institute.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates will have advanced knowledge of educational theory, which they will apply to professional contexts in education or related fields. They will have the skills to critically evaluate educational literature and empirical research to inform their professional practice. They will understand key challenges facing education and related fields and draw on literature and evidence to engage with identifying and implementing solutions to those challenges. Graduates will be able to take on senior roles within their professional contexts.","developedBy":"Victoria University of Wellington","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=VI001U"},"CN0946":{"i":"CN0946","entryRequirements":"A Bachelor's degree plus a recognised teacher training qualification or experience.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates will have knowledge of the history and current state of technology-enhanced language learning and of theories and practices in second language acquisition and in language teaching and learning. They will understand the affordances and constraints of digital tools and materials for language learning and critically evaluate the role these play in a variety of language learning contexts. Graduates will be able to design and implement language learning activities, experiences, lessons, units and courses including the judicious application of technology where appropriate. They will further be able to critically evaluate and advise on the application of existing and continuously emerging technology in learning environments. Graduates will have enhanced employability in digitally prepared education settings and they will be welcomed as innovators and entrepreneurs who can bring their skills and knowledge of learning environments. Expertise in technology-enhanced language learning means that graduates will be able to create learning opportunities for language learning, linking learners with competent speakers of target languages even in geographically remote communities","developedBy":"University of Canterbury","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=CN0946"},"VI001V":{"i":"VI001V","entryRequirements":"A bachelors degree with a major in education, psychology or a related discipline, or an approved teacher education degree, or a bachelors degree and a teaching diploma from an approved teacher education institute.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates will have knowledge of educational theory and will be able to apply this knowledge to professional contexts of education or related fields. They will be able to evaluate educational literature and empirical research. They will understand key challenges facing education and related fields and draw on literature and evidence to identify solutions.","developedBy":"Victoria University of Wellington","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=VI001V"},"OO0491":{"i":"OO0491","entryRequirements":"Completion of a bachelor's degree or postgraduate qualification to an appropriate standard.","strategicPurposeStatement":"At the end of the period of study participants will have developed an advanced knowledge of teaching and learning and be independent researchers of their own practices. They will understand the theories of teaching and student learning and how these apply to their own professional situations, across their institutions and in different disciplines and cultures. They will have leadership and mentoring skills and be able to guide other staff in issues pertaining to teaching and learning and higher education more broadly. A graduate will have a strong and critical sense of values and how these are developed and acquired by students.","developedBy":"University of Otago","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=OO0491"},"WI0971":{"i":"WI0971","entryRequirements":"Bachelors degree or equivalent and to have met the Teaching Council's selection criteria for ITE programme.","strategicPurposeStatement":"The Postgraduate Diploma in Teaching (PGDipTchg) is a 150-point programme of one calendar year that leads to registration and provisional certification as a teacher in New Zealand. University of Waikato graduates in Initial Teacher Education understand their world, their contribution to the future wellbeing of our society, and the influence that they can have on their learners. The PGDipTchg will allow students to learn at a higher level than the current Graduate Diploma of Teaching (GradDipTchg) and provides a pathway to the Master of Teaching and Learning (MTchgLn).","developedBy":"University of Waikato","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=WI0971"},"AU0164":{"i":"AU0164","entryRequirements":"Bachelor's degree in Education or approved equivalent qualification.  If enrolling in Counselling not less than 3 years' experience in a related area, an interview, referees' reports and police clearance are required.","strategicPurposeStatement":"PGDipEd graduates will have specialist skills in a selected area of education and will have an advanced understanding of the theoretical and practical contexts relating to this area.","developedBy":"University of Auckland","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=AU0164"},"MY0212":{"i":"MY0212","entryRequirements":"A bachelors degree, or equivalent; and at least one year full-time relevant teaching experience, or equivalent.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Holders will have advanced knowledge and skills in the area of second language teaching","developedBy":"Massey University","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=MY0212"},"AU0257":{"i":"AU0257","entryRequirements":"Any undergraduate degree or evidence of adequate training and/or experience to undertake the programme, and at least two years' teaching experience.","strategicPurposeStatement":"PGDipLangTchg graduates will be confident practitioners specialising in teaching a second or foreign language or in teaching other languages.","developedBy":"University of Auckland","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=AU0257"},"WI0805":{"i":"WI0805","entryRequirements":"BTchg or a bachelors degree and teaching qualification, or equivalent. Those students who have a BTchg(Hons), an honours degree and a teaching qualification or a PGDip in a relevant field or equivalent, may take 120 credits to complete the MEd.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates will have advanced understanding and knowledge about the body of theoretical and applied topics relevant to Education. They will have the qualities necessary for employment requiring the exercise of personal responsibility and decision-making.  They will contribute to New Zealands development in Education, in specific areas such as curriculum and specialist subject studies, early childhood education, educational leadership, Maori education, adult community and tertiary education, counselling, developmental and educational psychology, and special education.","developedBy":"University of Waikato","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=WI0805"},"OO0322":{"i":"OO0322","entryRequirements":"Bachelors degree or equivalent qualification. Registration to teach in New Zealand schools and relevant experience","strategicPurposeStatement":"This diploma course produces knowledgeable and critically reflective teaching practitioners who are able to enhance the quality of student learning outcomes by using information and communication technology.","developedBy":"University of Otago","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=OO0322"},"AK1062":{"i":"AK1062","entryRequirements":"Bachelor's degree or equivalent.  In exceptional circumstances an appropriate combination of academic and professional experience deemed equivalent to a bachelor's degree may be considered.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates of the Postgraduate Certificate in Higher Education will demonstrate an advanced knowledge and level of reflection on their practice, which prepares them for further postgraduate study.","developedBy":"Auckland University of Technology","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=AK1062"},"MY0161":{"i":"MY0161","entryRequirements":"Bachelor's degree, a recognised teaching qualification, and relevant professional experience.","strategicPurposeStatement":"The postgraduate Diploma in Learning Support aims to provide SENCO/School Leaders of Learning Support with a programme which integrates theory and practice to explore key aspects of the SENCO role. It aims to extend SENCO/School Leaders of Learning Supports' knowledge and skills in relation to their role and provide them with a space and place to learn with, from and about other SENCO/School Leaders of Learning Support throughout New Zealand to improve their practice. Overall this programme aims to support a strong SENCO network throughout New Zealand and improve services to students who have special needs.","developedBy":"Massey University","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=MY0161"},"OO0012":{"i":"OO0012","entryRequirements":"Bachelor's degree or acceptable alternative.  Preferred qualifications are Bachelor of Education, Bachelor of Arts in education, or a postgraduate diploma in education.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates will have advanced knowledge in the field, and the ability to study independently and to carry out original research to internationally recognised standards.","developedBy":"University of Otago","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=OO0012"},"AU5542":{"i":"AU5542","entryRequirements":"A Bachelor of Education (Teaching), a Bachelor of Arts in Education, or an equivalent qualification, or an approved professional qualification in education and at least two years of relevant professional experience.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates will have a critical understanding of key concepts and debates in the field of education, including broad conceptual and theoretical elements. They will have to capacity for critical, conceptual and reflective thinking in the field of education at postgraduate level.","graduateProfile":"Not applicable","educationPathway":"Not applicable","developedBy":"University of Auckland","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=AU5542"},"CN0924":{"i":"CN0924","entryRequirements":"Master of Education endorsed in Child and Family Psychology, or an approved Master's degree or PhD including specified papers.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates can work effectively with children, their families and their professional caregivers. They will have undertaken focused study and training to become skilled professionals able to work in relevant community and educational settings.","developedBy":"University of Canterbury","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=CN0924"},"VI00Z2":{"i":"VI00Z2","entryRequirements":"This programme has both theoretical and practicum work in the field of educational psychology. Students complete 1500 hours of supervised practice in educational and community based settings. Courses include professional identity, ethical, and legal issues; and specialist assessment and intervention in educational and community-based settings.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates will have the knowledge and skills to work in education and community-related contexts as educational psychologists.  As specialists in interventions and assessment, they will engage in evidence-based practices in the field of educational psychology and inclusive education. Graduates have the academic requirements to apply for registration as psychologists within the Education Scope of Practice with the New Zealand Psychologists Board.  They will become educational psychologists or leaders in other areas of the education sector.  Some graduates may be able to continue to a PhD.","developedBy":"Victoria University of Wellington","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=VI00Z2"},"CE1060":{"i":"CE1060","entryRequirements":"Applicants must hold a degree of relevance to clinical teaching or hold an appropriate health or allied professional qualification or equivalent.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Health professional graduates will have developed their skills and knowledge as educators, applying current research to their teaching role and researching best practice.","developedBy":"University of Canterbury","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=CE1060"},"OO0236":{"i":"OO0236","entryRequirements":"A bachelor's degree and current employment as a tertiary teacher.","strategicPurposeStatement":"This diploma programme provides study, practical training and an introduction to research in designated areas of tertiary teaching. Recipients of the diploma can proceed to further postgraduate study.","developedBy":"University of Otago","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=OO0236"},"MY0181":{"i":"MY0181","entryRequirements":"Bachelor's degree, a recognised teaching qualification, and relevant professional experience.","strategicPurposeStatement":"The proposed qualifications aim to provide SENCO/School Leaders of Learning Support with a programme which integrates theory and practice to explore key aspects of the SENCO role. It aims to extend SENCO/School Leaders of Learning Supports' knowledge and skills in relation to their role and provide them with a space and place to learn with, from and about other SENCO/School Leaders of Learning Support throughout New Zealand to improve their practice. Overall this programme aims to support a strong SENCO network throughout New Zealand and improve services to students who have special needs.","developedBy":"Massey University","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=MY0181"},"AK1052":{"i":"AK1052","entryRequirements":"A bachelors degree or a professional qualification or experience equivalent to a bachelors degree","strategicPurposeStatement":"PgCertEdL graduates will demonstrate an advanced level of understanding of selected issues in educational leadership. They will be competent and reflective practitioners and be prepared for further postgraduate study.","developedBy":"Auckland University of Technology","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=AK1052"},"CN1905":{"i":"CN1905","entryRequirements":"An approved Bachelor's degree or equivalent.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates of the Postgraduate Certificate in Educational Psychology (PGCertEdPsyc) will be able to describe and apply key concepts, principles, and theories in educational psychology. They will have a strong working knowledge of learning sciences, assessment, behaviour, and inclusion, integrating insights from psychology and education. They will critically evaluate research and evidence-based practices, using scientific reasoning and methods to analyse and interpret educational data and phenomena.  Graduates will engage in integrative thinking and problem-solving, applying psychological principles to enhance teaching, learning, and student well-being. They will demonstrate ethical awareness in educational settings and incorporate bicultural confidence and competence, recognising their obligations under Te Tiriti o Waitangi. They will be able to communicate effectively in verbal and written formats across diverse professional and academic contexts, equipping them to contribute meaningfully to education, policy, and leadership in Aotearoa New Zealand and beyond.","graduateProfile":"A range of assessments will be used in coursework.","developedBy":"University of Canterbury","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=CN1905"},"OO0470":{"i":"OO0470","entryRequirements":"An appropriate Masters degree, Postgraduate diploma or Bachelors degree.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates will have extended their skills in research and evaluation contributing to professional knowledge and practice.","developedBy":"University of Otago","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=OO0470"},"AK1051":{"i":"AK1051","entryRequirements":"A bachelors degree or graduate diploma or a professional qualification or experience deemed equivalent to a bachelors degree.","strategicPurposeStatement":"MEdL graduates will have enhanced leadership capabilities, and be able to undertake or continue leadership roles in educational organisations (from the ECE to tertiary sectors), government, and corporate settings; enhanced understanding of leadership issues in their own and other educational settings; be able to respond effectively to leadership issues facing a diverse range of students/teachers from diverse cultural backgrounds; use theory to direct practice and use practice to formulate theory; engage in reflective, ethical and effective learning and teaching practice in relation to educational leadership.","developedBy":"Auckland University of Technology","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=AK1051"},"VI002S":{"i":"VI002S","entryRequirements":"A master's degree or bachelor's degree with honours in education and a minimum of five years' experience in a relevant professional role.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates will be able to conduct high-level, independent research on a professional issue and report their findings as an integrated report. They will acquire the advanced knowledge and research skills to lead and contribute to initiatives to improve educational policy and practice.","graduateProfile":"NA","educationPathway":"NA","developedBy":"Victoria University of Wellington","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=VI002S"},"WI1008":{"i":"WI1008","strategicPurposeStatement":"This Masters degree develops fundamental areas of knowledge and practice for both present and future teachers of English to speakers of other languages, specifically: knowledge of theories and research in: second language acquisition, pedagogic linguistics, language assessment, second language pedagogy and TESOL research methodologies.","developedBy":"University of Waikato","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=WI1008"},"AU5538":{"i":"AU5538","entryRequirements":"A bachelors degree, bachelor honours degree, masters degree, graduate diploma or postgraduate diploma with a grade point average of 5 or higher.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates of the MTchg(Primary) will possess the knowledge, skills and dispositions to make a positive difference for learners from priority student groups. They will be critical, self-motivated, resilient and collaborative practitioners. Graduates will have a critical understanding of how to conduct an investigation and use research and evidence to inform and develop their professional knowledge and practice.","developedBy":"University of Auckland","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=AU5538"},"VI001M":{"i":"VI001M","entryRequirements":"A bachelors degree in a relevant learning area with a grade point average of at least a B in the final year of study. Preference may be given to applicants holding tertiary qualifications that include science, te reo Mâori and/or mathematics.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates will have advanced knowledge of the professional practice of evidence-based teaching and curriculum implementation in the primary school context. They will have the skills and knowledge to establish and sustain a positive, inclusive learning environment and will be able to evaluate their own effectiveness using evidence of student learning progress. They will be able to critique research literature, teaching practices, and educational policy and will use their knowledge to develop the learning potential of all of their students. Graduates will be eligible to apply for teacher registration in New Zealand or progress to doctoral study with additional research methods study.","developedBy":"Victoria University of Wellington","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=VI001M"},"VI00X4":{"i":"VI00X4","entryRequirements":"A bachelor's degree","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates will apply their operational knowledge of finance to improve financial outcomes in their chosen area of employment. They will be able to work as corporate and bank financial risk analysts, dealers, professional back office staff, as financial analysts in the areas of funds management, corporate banking, corporate financial planning, stockbroking, financial communications and data provision, government and local body, management consultancy and industry regulation. Graduates may be able to progress to further study in the Postgraduate Diploma in Financial Analysis.","developedBy":"Victoria University of Wellington","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=VI00X4"},"CN0936":{"i":"CN0936","entryRequirements":"An approved Bachelor's degree and a teaching qualification.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates will be able to critically examine and apply theory and research and apply the principles of biculturalism in Aotearoa New Zealand to inform and sustain culturally responsive practice. They will be able to critically reflect on the influence of global conditions, author debates and their connection to their educational practice. They will have enhanced and extended their educational practice through the development of knowledge, skills and strategies and be able to use evidence to inform and justify new developments in their educational practice.","developedBy":"University of Canterbury","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=CN0936"},"VI001A":{"i":"VI001A","entryRequirements":"A bachelors degree with honours or a Postgraduate Certificate in Higher Education Learning and Teaching from Victoria University of Wellington (VI00R8), or equivalent professional or scholarly experience of an appropriate kind","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates will have will have developed the mastery level skills required for designing and carrying out ethical and informative educational research, of a publishable standard. They will have developed advanced skills in articulating, challenging and developing personal theories of teaching and learning in relation to diverse educational literature and theory. They will be able to design intended learning outcomes, activities and assessments, using appropriate theory and research, in ways which address relevant characteristics of diverse student populations in modern universities, and to reflect upon and critically evaluate evidence of teaching effectiveness and to apply resulting knowledge to improve their teaching and students learning, and to support such changes within their disciplines and institutions. The PGDipHELT will enhance graduates career development within academia, and may lead to greater employability in the higher education sector in countries where professional teaching qualifications are required. Graduates will be prepared to adopt stronger academic leadership roles in teaching development within their institutions. Some graduates may be able to continue to masters level or doctoral studies in higher education. Graduates will have will have developed the mastery level skills required for designing and carrying out ethical and informative educational research, of a publishable standard. They will have developed advanced skills in articulating, challenging and developing personal theories of teaching and learning in relation to diverse educational literature and theory. They will be able to design intended learning outcomes, activities and assessments, using appropriate theory and research, in ways which address relevant characteristics of diverse student populations in modern universities, and to reflect upon and critically evaluate evidence of teaching effectiveness and to apply resulting knowledge to improve their teaching and students lea","developedBy":"Victoria University of Wellington","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=VI001A"},"CN0916":{"i":"CN0916","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates will be able to effectively design and implement the teaching of English as an additional language in a variety of language learning contexts through critical discussion of current research, design, evaluation and implementation of digital and traditional teaching materials and examination of practice and planning. Graduates will have developed a critical awareness and understanding of the current situation of English in a globalized world and of principles and practices of learning and teaching English in the 21st century and how these can be applied in real learning environments.","graduateProfile":"Graduates will be able to effectively design and implement the teaching of English as an additional language in a variety of language learning contexts through critical discussion of current research, design, evaluation and implementation of digital and traditional teaching materials and examination of practice and planning. Graduates will have developed a critical awareness and understanding of the current situation of English in a globalized world and of principles and practices of learning and teaching English in the 21st century and how these can be applied in real learning environments.","developedBy":"University of Canterbury","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=CN0916"},"AK3685":{"i":"AK3685","entryRequirements":"A master's degree in education, or other master's or bachelor's degree with at least second-class honours in a relevant discipline; at least 5 years' professional experience in education or a related area.","strategicPurposeStatement":"EdD graduates will have developed a very broad range of research and applied skills together with advanced analytical and critical thinking skills. They will be capable of demonstrating high levels of independence, professionalism, and leadership in their chosen field. They will achieve a high degree of research expertise in the topic area of their doctoral work. Graduates will also have further developed their personal capabilities and will be well placed to construct and apply advanced theoretical concepts and knowledge to investigate professional practice in a wide range of domains.","developedBy":"Auckland University of Technology","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=AK3685"},"WI0051":{"i":"WI0051","entryRequirements":"BEd degree or equivalent","strategicPurposeStatement":"This 2 year version of the MEd programme is no longer offered. The University now offers a 1 year MEd building on a BTchg(Hons) degree. Please refer to the University of Waikato website for further details & other options. \nhere","developedBy":"University of Waikato","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=WI0051"},"WI0159":{"i":"WI0159","entryRequirements":"PGDipEdLeadership or equivalent","strategicPurposeStatement":"For more information on this qualification, please refer to the University of Waikato's website. \nhere","developedBy":"University of Waikato","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=WI0159"},"CA2292":{"i":"CA2292","entryRequirements":"Applicants must have a relevant Bachelors degree with merit achievement, (B- or higher) or a relevant equivalent qualification, or be able to demonstrate equivalent competencies.  There is an English language requirement.  Contact Unitec for full details.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates from this programme will gain a sound understanding of a number of aspects of educational theory, policy and practice.","developedBy":"Manukau Institute of Technology and Unitec - Unitec","qualityAssuredBy":"Quality Assurance Division, NZQA","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=CA2292"},"CN904":{"i":"CN904","entryRequirements":"Any appropriate degree in New Zealand either including advanced courses in education with a total value of at least 56 credits or with a recognised teacher training course of not less than one year at a New Zealand college of education or university.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates will receive an advanced introduction to research-based understanding and knowledge of contemporary educational theory and professional practice. The certificate emphasises critical thinking about and understanding of issues across a limited range of educational areas.","developedBy":"University of Canterbury","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=CN904"},"WI0893":{"i":"WI0893","entryRequirements":"BTchg, BSocSc or a bachelors degree and teaching qualification, or equivalent. Those students who have a PGDip in Disability and Inclusion Studies or equivalent, may take 120 credits to complete the MDInS.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates will have advanced understanding and knowledge about the specialist body of theoretical and applied topics relevant to disability and  inclusion studies. They will understand the underlying concepts and principles associated with disability and inclusion studies , the main methods of enquiry and the different approaches to solving problems in disability and inclusion studies.","developedBy":"University of Waikato","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=WI0893"},"WI0806":{"i":"WI0806","entryRequirements":"BTchg or a bachelors degree and teaching qualification, or equivalent. Those students who have a BTchg(Hons), an honours degree and a teaching qualification or a PGDip in Educational Leadership or equivalent, may take 120 credits to complete the MEdLeadership.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates will have advanced understanding and knowledge about the specialist body of theoretical and applied topics relevant to educational leadership. They will contribute to the social and economic development, labour market and community of New Zealand by providing innovative educational leadership and management practice in schools, tertiary institutions, Government departments, and non-Governmental organisations.","developedBy":"University of Waikato","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=WI0806"},"WI0474":{"i":"WI0474","entryRequirements":"Honours or Masters degree with at least second class honours (first division), and a recognised teaching or allied professional qualification with at least three years service.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates will have developed a sustained, rigorous and systematic approach to the investigation, development, and theorisation of professional practice in educational settings. They will have an in-depth knowledge and understanding of the theoretical bases of empirical studies and clinical practices in the relevant field of study to a level beyond that normally attained in masters programmes.","developedBy":"University of Waikato","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=WI0474"},"OO0422":{"i":"OO0422","entryRequirements":"Bachelor's degree or equivalent and 3 years' successful teaching experience","strategicPurposeStatement":"This degree produces knowledgeable and critically reflective teaching practitioners who are able to enhance the quality of student learning outcomes.","developedBy":"University of Otago","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=OO0422"},"MY0259":{"i":"MY0259","entryRequirements":"For entry into Te Aho Paerewa candidates are required to: hold a Bachelor's degree with at least a B grade average across the 200 & 300 level papers, or equivalent: be a fluent speaker of Te Reo Māori; have demonstrated competence in literacy and numeracy in Te Reo Māori; meet the requirements for registration as set down by the EDUCANZ in terms of good character and fitness to be a teacher; be selected into the programme following a selection process which may include: Police Vetting, a written application, interview, and written and/or practical exercises.","strategicPurposeStatement":"On successful completion of Te aho Paerewa (Post Graduate Diploma Teaching & Learning in Māori Medium) graduates will have developed the exertise to meet the needs of learners enrolled in the Kura Kaupapa Māori system.  Candidates will be able to teach in the compulsory primary schooling sector generally and Kura Kaupapa Māori in particular.","graduateProfile":"Not applicable","educationPathway":"Not applicable","developedBy":"Massey University","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=MY0259"},"VI00R8":{"i":"VI00R8","entryRequirements":"Bachelor's degree with Honours","strategicPurposeStatement":"Assist new and experienced tertiary-level teaching staff become more reflective, effective, and successful in practicing and/or supporting research-led learning and teaching.","developedBy":"Victoria University of Wellington","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=VI00R8"},"AU5586":{"i":"AU5586","entryRequirements":"A bachelor's degree and an approved graduate or postgraduate diploma; or a Bachelor of Education (Teaching) (Honours);  and a minimum Grade Point Average (GPA).  Additional entry requirements apply for entry to the 120-point pathway.","strategicPurposeStatement":"MEdPrac graduates will be teachers who value, understand and apply proven pedagogical approaches for improving student learning. They will be teachers who will have the skills to undertake such approaches and are engaged in practicing in this way to improve student learning in their own educational contexts.","developedBy":"University of Auckland","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=AU5586"},"CN0937":{"i":"CN0937","entryRequirements":"An approved Bachelor's degree","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates will promote leadership and raise the knowledge of te reo Maori in bilingual, immersion, and mainstream settings. Hoaka Pounamu | Maori Bilingual and Immersion Teaching and Learning is designed to extend teachers on the use of te reo Maori in the classroom.","graduateProfile":"A range of assessments will be used including essays, literature reviews, other written assignments and reports, and seminar presentations.","developedBy":"University of Canterbury","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=CN0937"},"MY1050":{"i":"MY1050","entryRequirements":"Bachelor of Education or Bachelor of Education (Teaching); or bachelor's degree plus appropriate professional qualification; or equivalent.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates will have specialised professional expertise at the postgraduate level.","developedBy":"Massey University","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=MY1050"},"CN1055":{"i":"CN1055","entryRequirements":"A New Zealand teacher education degree or a recognised three year diploma in early childhood, primary or secondary teaching or equivalent qualification and/or relevant professional experience.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates will have a strong appreciation of specialist teaching as an interdisciplinary field of practice and in-depth knowledge and skills in their endorsed area of teaching. They will also have a thorough understanding of how to apply their specialist teaching knowledge and skills in diverse cultural contexts.","developedBy":"University of Canterbury","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=CN1055"},"MY0162":{"i":"MY0162","entryRequirements":"A degree in teaching; or any bachelors degree and a professional teaching qualification; or equivalent.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates will have specialised postgraduate understandings and skills which will enable them to exercise professional leadership.","developedBy":"Massey University","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=MY0162"},"CN0925":{"i":"CN0925","entryRequirements":"An approved Bachelor's degree","strategicPurposeStatement":"This qualification will produce students who are skilful and knowledgeable English language educators for a variety of settings in New Zealand and internationally. They will have developed knowledge about language acquisition and development, as well as skills in teaching languages, and understanding of the linguistic and social pressures experienced by learners and users of indigenous, heritage or additional languages. Students will have gained a comprehensive overview of the field of language learning and teaching, and the ability to judiciously plan for research-informed language courses for a variety of settings, target languages, learners and learning environments.","developedBy":"University of Canterbury","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=CN0925"},"MY1051":{"i":"MY1051","entryRequirements":"Bachelor's degree or equivalent; teaching in a tertiary institution; professional experience.","strategicPurposeStatement":"PGCertTT graduates will construct personal knowledge about tertiary teaching; integrate into practice conceptual and theoretical understandings; develop an ethical basis for practice; critique and improve their practice; cater for diverse student needs.","developedBy":"Massey University","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=MY1051"},"AU5646":{"i":"AU5646","entryRequirements":"A relevant Bachelors degree or an Advanced Diploma in Teaching or a professional qualification in teaching with at least two years of prior professional experience.","strategicPurposeStatement":"PGCertTLDL graduates will understand the principles of Second Language Acquisition and Bilingual/Plurilingual Education and be able to enhance the learning, capabilities and outcomes of all learners, in particular those from diverse language backgrounds.","developedBy":"University of Auckland","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=AU5646"},"AU5525":{"i":"AU5525","entryRequirements":"Bachelors degree with an average grade of B or higher or equivalent and 30 points at 300-400 level in a subject within the secondary school curriculum. Demonstrates potential to meet New Zealand Teachers Council criteria for provisional registration.","strategicPurposeStatement":"PGDipTchg (Secondary Field-based) graduates will know and understand the principles, concepts and ideas of current theory and research about effective teaching and learning. They will have a sound understanding of the national curriculum and the national qualifications framework. Graduates will be able to critically apply this knowledge to practice in schools, and be able to further develop coherence between educational theory, research, and practice. They will contribute effectively to raising student achievement. Graduates will be self-aware and researching professionals, who use critical inquiry and problem solving, and who engage with evidence and professional literature to reflect on and refine their practice. They will have developed the knowledge and skills to contribute to informed discussion of equitable education in the wider community.","developedBy":"University of Auckland","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=AU5525"},"AU5602":{"i":"AU5602","entryRequirements":"Have completed the requirements for an approved degree, and b) be currently employed in the tertiary education sector with a substantial role in teaching or supporting student learning, or have been employed in such a role within the past three years.","strategicPurposeStatement":"MHigherEd graduates will be equipped to teach in a way that enhances the learning, capabilities and graduate outcomes of all their students; to innovate and exhibit leadership in their teaching; and to foster a community of practice that enables them to develop and share their practice with colleagues and enhance the standard and standing of tertiary teaching at their institution and beyond.","developedBy":"University of Auckland","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=AU5602"},"OO0323":{"i":"OO0323","entryRequirements":"A bachelor's degree or equivalent; registration to teach in New Zealand schools; and relevant experience.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Holders of this certificate are knowledgeable and critically reflective practitioners who are able to enhance the quality of student learning outcomes by using information and communication technology.","developedBy":"University of Otago","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=OO0323"},"VI001B":{"i":"VI001B","entryRequirements":"A bachelors degree and experience in middle or senior management in a secondary school.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates will have the knowledge and capabilities to undertake leadership roles in New Zealand secondary schools, as school principals. They will be critically reflective and ethical leaders who resource and develop capacity to improve learning outcomes for a diverse range of students. They will be able to undertake independent research in fields relevant to secondary school leadership.","developedBy":"Victoria University of Wellington","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=VI001B"},"VI004A":{"i":"VI004A","entryRequirements":"A bachelor's degree in a relevant subject area with an average of B or better along with at least two years of related professional experience.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates of the PGDipTESOL will demonstrate advanced knowledge of core topics in the teaching of second languages, the nature of language and the nature of second language learning. They will be able to engage with the relevant literature critically and creatively and will be able to apply this knowledge effectively to teaching practice and to communication about language teaching and learning. Graduates from the PGDipTESOL may be able to continue to the MA in TESOL.","developedBy":"Victoria University of Wellington","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=VI004A"},"AU0394":{"i":"AU0394","entryRequirements":"Postgraduate Diploma in Education, Bachelor of Education (Teaching) (Honours), Bachelor of Education (Teaching), or Bachelor of Arts or an equivalent qualification.","strategicPurposeStatement":"MEd graduates will be skilled and reflective practitioners with an understanding of current educational issues and an ability to conduct advanced research in a chosen area of teaching practice","developedBy":"University of Auckland","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=AU0394"},"AU5162":{"i":"AU5162","entryRequirements":"A bachelors degree. Applicants must be currently employed in tertiary education with significant teaching and/or student learning support roles.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Postgraduate Certificate of Academic Practice graduates will have a scholarly understanding of teaching, learning and academic practice within their disciplines; capacity for self-reflection and continuing professional development; a sound knowledge of current research and theories in the field of tertiary teaching and learning; an ability to apply higher education theory to their academic practice; a familiarity with relevant institutional and national policies; a collaborative, collegial approach to teaching, research and service.","developedBy":"University of Auckland","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=AU5162"},"MY0396":{"i":"MY0396","entryRequirements":"University degree in education or psychology; or equivalent; professional experience.","strategicPurposeStatement":"MEdPsych degree graduates will have the foundation skills and knowledge for work in the field of educational psychology and special education. Graduates will demonstrate knowledge and understanding of theory and research in educational psychology.","developedBy":"Massey University","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=MY0396"},"CN0024":{"i":"CN0024","entryRequirements":"Applicants must be practising tertiary teachers and have either a degree or equivalent qualifications or experience.","strategicPurposeStatement":"The programme produces effective, professional tertiary teachers. Central is the notion of teacher-practitioner as lifelong learner. The programme adopts a learner-centred experiential approach situated within the participants' own professional work.","developedBy":"University of Canterbury","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=CN0024"},"OO0533":{"i":"OO0533","entryRequirements":"A bachelor's degree with an average grade of at least B+ in the final year of study, or equivalent, and three years' successful teaching experience.","strategicPurposeStatement":"This master's degree programme is for professionals already working in the teaching and/or education sector. Completing this qualification will enable teachers to compete with new graduates, and to show their advanced knowledge in their chosen profession.","developedBy":"University of Otago","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=OO0533"},"AU5506":{"i":"AU5506","entryRequirements":"A bachelor's degree, a professional qualification in teaching, and have at least three years practical experience in teaching or in a related profession, including experience in a formal or informal leadership and/or management role.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates of the PGDipEdLd will have knowledge, skills, and experience in the field of educational leadership. They will be critical, reflective leaders who can make a positive difference to learning outcomes for students and teachers across education sectors.","developedBy":"University of Auckland","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=AU5506"},"AU5526":{"i":"AU5526","entryRequirements":"Enrolled in a Doctor of Philosophy or Doctor of Medicine for at least 12 months or completed the research requirements for Doctor of Education and recommended for admission by their Head of Department and Faculty Dean or nominee.","strategicPurposeStatement":"MPhil graduates will have successfully completed a research thesis (under supervision) through which they will have shown evidence of advanced knowledge about a specialist field of enquiry and developed and demonstrated advanced academic and intellectual skills. They will be able to address new questions with a rigorous, critical, evidence-based approach.","developedBy":"University of Auckland","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=AU5526"},"VI001D":{"i":"VI001D","entryRequirements":"A bachelors degree and experience in middle or senior management in a secondary school.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates will be able to demonstrate understanding of educational leadership at the postgraduate level, enabling them to exercise professional leadership within a secondary school environment. They may continue to the Postgraduate Diploma in Secondary School Leadership (VI001C) and the Master of Secondary School Leadership (VI001B).","developedBy":"Victoria University of Wellington","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=VI001D"},"CN0966":{"i":"CN0966","entryRequirements":"A Bachelor's degree.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates will be critical pedagogues, action competent and culturally responsive. They will have advanced research-based knowledge and integrated understanding and experiences of contemporary educational theory and professional practice. They will be highly knowledgeable and skilled teachers who are innovative, adaptable and resilient in supporting and enhancing the diverse learning strengths of each of their students in ways that result in positive educational outcomes. They will be committed to and skilful in, developing and sustaining partnerships with family, whanau, hapu, iwi, aiga and community and other education and health professionals. They will meet the requirements for provisional registration with the New Zealand Teachers Council.","developedBy":"University of Canterbury","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=CN0966"},"MY0394":{"i":"MY0394","entryRequirements":"Bachelor of Education; or undergraduate degree plus professional teaching qualification; or equivalent.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates will have acquired advanced professional knowledge and capabilities and investigated new developments in professional education and/or related fields.","developedBy":"Massey University","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=MY0394"},"AK1061":{"i":"AK1061","entryRequirements":"Bachelor's degree or equivalent plus at least two years of education practice experience.","strategicPurposeStatement":"PgDipEdPrac graduates will have advanced knowledge and skills in reflective educational practice to the extent that they will be able to pursue professional goals and further postgraduate study. The emphasis placed on integrating theory and practice will enhance problem solving and analytical skills.","developedBy":"Auckland University of Technology","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=AK1061"},"CN0955":{"i":"CN0955","entryRequirements":"An approved Bachelor's degree.","strategicPurposeStatement":"The programme will provide formal recognition for people wishing to add counselling skills to their repertoire alongside complementary professional qualifications, for example in education or health.","developedBy":"University of Canterbury","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=CN0955"},"OO0292":{"i":"OO0292","entryRequirements":"A bachelor's degree and current employment as a tertiary teacher","strategicPurposeStatement":"This certificate programme provides study, practical training and an introduction to research in designated areas of tertiary teaching. Recipients of the certificate can proceed to further postgraduate study.","developedBy":"University of Otago","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=OO0292"},"CN0918":{"i":"CN0918","entryRequirements":"A Bachelor's degree or Master's degree plus normally a minimum of five years' experience in a professional role","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates will be able to: link, in a coherent and rigorous manner, theory and research to questions of professional practice; meet high international standards in the quality of their scholarly work; be adept at presenting their work in conferences and seminars and in publishing their findings; value the process of peer review in advancing scholarly and professional understanding; adopt an investigative, probing, open-minded, reflective, dialogical stand in addressing scholarly and professional problems; draw on insights from their studies in seeking to make a worthwhile difference in the lives of others; be willing and able to demonstrate leadership in their domains of professional endeavour and have a strong sense of global awareness and participate actively in wider scholarly and professional communities.","developedBy":"University of Canterbury","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=CN0918"},"OO0370":{"i":"OO0370","entryRequirements":"Bachelor of Teaching (Primary) compelted at a high standard with exemplary performance in teaching practicums","strategicPurposeStatement":"This provides a fourth year of prescribed study to undergraduate preservice teacher education students.","developedBy":"University of Otago","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=OO0370"},"CN0906":{"i":"CN0906","entryRequirements":"Bachelor's degree","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates will have an advanced understanding of educational research and thought in relation to areas of professional interest and practice.","developedBy":"University of Canterbury","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=CN0906"},"VI0043":{"i":"VI0043","entryRequirements":"A bachelor's degree with a major in education, psychology or a related discipline, or an approved teacher education degree, or a bachelor's degree and teaching diploma from an approved teacher education institute.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates will have advanced knowledge of educational theory and research which they will apply to professional contexts in education or related fields. They will have the skills to critically evaluate educational literature and empirical research and to gather, analyse and use evidence to understand, inform and enhance professional practice. They will understand key challenges facing education and related fields and draw on literature and evidence to engage with identifying and implementing solutions to those challenges. They will be able to undertake individual research and apply their research skills to new situations. Graduates will be well prepared to take on senior and leadership roles within their professional contexts.  Some graduates will be eligible to progress to doctoral study. This 180-point version of the MEd replaces the 120 point MEd (VI0043), phased out from 2015.","developedBy":"Victoria University of Wellington","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=VI0043"},"AU5604":{"i":"AU5604","entryRequirements":"Have completed the requirements for an approved degree, and b) be currently employed in the tertiary education sector with a substantial role in teaching or supporting student learning, or have been employed in such a role within the past three years.","strategicPurposeStatement":"PGCertHigherEd graduates will be equipped to teach in a way that enhances the learning, capabilities and graduate outcomes of all their students; to innovate in their teaching; and to foster a community of practice that enables them to develop and share their practice with colleagues.","developedBy":"University of Auckland","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=AU5604"}};
